), and  \n   - a vertical tile covering rows \\(i_j+1,\\dots ,n\\) (if this set is non‑empty).  \n\n   These tiles are disjoint, each lies on grid lines, and together they cover every unit square except the \\(n\\) uncovered ones.  \n   The total number of tiles used is exactly the number of non‑empty intervals, i.e. \\(2n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\((i,j)\\) the unit square in row \\(i\\) and column \\(j\\) \\((1\\le i,j\\le n)\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, there exists a permutation  \n\n\\[\np\\colon\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nEquivalently, for each column \\(j\\) there is a unique row  \n\n\\[\ni_j:=p^{-1}(j)\n\\]\n\nwith the uncovered square \\((i_j,j)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The maximal rectangles of the complement  \n\nFix a column \\(j\\).  The cells of this column that have to be covered are\n\n\\[\nA_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<i_j\\},\\qquad  \nB_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i_j<i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(A_j\\) (resp. \\(B_j\\)) is called the *above* (resp. *below*) interval of column \\(j\\).  \nIf \\(i_j=1\\) then \\(A_j=\\varnothing\\); if \\(i_j=n\\) then \\(B_j=\\varnothing\\).\n\nObserve that a rectangle whose interior contains two cells of the same column must contain the whole vertical segment between them.  Consequently a rectangle that avoids all uncovered squares cannot contain both \\(A_j\\) and \\(B_j\\) for the same column \\(j\\); otherwise it would also contain the hole \\((i_j,j)\\).  Hence each of the sets \\(A_j\\) and \\(B_j\\) is a **maximal** rectangle of the complement: it cannot be enlarged in any direction without meeting a hole.\n\nSince the permutation \\(p\\) is a bijection, the numbers \\(|A_j|=i_j-1\\) run through the values \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) exactly once, and the numbers \\(|B_j|=n-i_j\\) also run through \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) exactly once.  Therefore\n\n* exactly one column has an empty above‑interval (\\(i_j=1\\));\n* exactly one column has an empty below‑interval (\\(i_j=n\\));\n* all other columns have both intervals non‑empty.\n\nConsequently the complement of the permutation matrix is the disjoint union of  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-2)}_{\\text{columns with both intervals}} \\times 2\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{2}_{\\text{columns with a single interval}}\n\\;=\\;2n-2\n\\]\n\nmaximal rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound  \n\nLet a tiling of the complement be given.  Each tile is a rectangle whose interior contains no hole.  As argued above, such a rectangle cannot intersect two different maximal rectangles of the same column; therefore it must be **contained** in a single maximal rectangle.  Hence every maximal rectangle needs at least one tile.  Because there are exactly \\(2n-2\\) maximal rectangles, any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge 2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor every column \\(j\\) we place the following tiles:\n\n* if \\(A_j\\neq\\varnothing\\) (i.e. \\(i_j>1\\)), place the vertical tile  \n\n  \\[\n  R^{\\text{up}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<i_j\\};\n  \\]\n\n* if \\(B_j\\neq\\varnothing\\) (i.e. \\(i_j<n\\)), place the vertical tile  \n\n  \\[\n  R^{\\text{down}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i_j<i\\le n\\}.\n  \\]\n\nEach tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines, the tiles are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every cell except the uncovered squares \\((i_j,j)\\).  The number of tiles used is precisely the number of non‑empty intervals, namely \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound of Section 2 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction of Section 3 shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048}.\n\\]"
