lar tiles (any size, aligned to grid lines) covering unit squares, each unit square covered at most once. We need each row and each column to have exactly one uncovered unit square. So there are exactly n uncovered squares (one per row, one per column). Since each column also has exactly one uncovered square, the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix (i.e., exactly one per row and column). So indeed p is a permutation.\n\nNow we need to cover all other squares (n^2 - n squares) with rectangles, each rectangle covering some contiguous block of squares (i.e., a rectangle). Tiles cannot overlap (since each unit square covered at most once). The goal is to minimize number of tiles.\n\nWe need to find minimal number of rectangles covering all squares except the uncovered ones.\n\nThe solution's lower bound argument: They partition the board into left region L (cells left of each uncovered square) and right region R (cells right). They argue each tile cannot cross from L to R because then it would cover the uncovered square. That's correct: If a rectangle includes a cell left of the hole and a cell right of the hole in the same row, then the rectangle's column interval must include the column of the hole, thus covering it, which is not allowed. However, could a rectangle cross from L to R but in different rows? For example, a rectangle could include a cell left of the hole in row i and a cell right of the hole in row j, but not include any cell in the same row crossing. But the rectangle's column interval is contiguous; if it includes column p(i)-1 (left of hole in row i) and column p(j)+1 (right of hole in row j), then the column interval must include all columns between those, which may include p(i) or p(j). But does that guarantee covering a hole? Let's examine.\n\nSuppose we have rows i and j, with holes at columns p(i) and p(j). Suppose a rectangle includes a cell (i, a) with a < p(i) (left side) and a cell (j, b) with b > p(j) (right side). The rectangle's column interval is from min(a,b) to max(a,b). Since a < p(i) and b > p(j), the interval includes the columns between a and b. Does it necessarily include p(i) or p(j)? Possibly not if p(i) > b or p(j) < a. But note a < p(i) and b > p(j). If p(i) <= b, then p(i) is in the interval. If p(j) >= a, then p(j) is in the interval. It's possible that p(i) > b and p(j) < a, but that would mean p(i) > b > a > p(j). But p(i) and p(j) are columns of holes; they could be anywhere. So it's possible that the rectangle includes left cell of row i and right cell of row j, but the column interval does not include either hole column. For example, suppose p(i) = 10, p(j) = 5, a = 3 (<10), b = 7 (>5). Then interval [3,7] includes column 5 (hole in row j) but not column 10. So it would cover the hole at (j,5). So not allowed. If we try to avoid covering any hole, we need to ensure that for each row i, the rectangle's column interval does not include p(i) if it includes any cell left of p(i) in that row, and similarly for rows where it includes cells right of p(i). But if rectangle includes cells from multiple rows, we need to ensure that for each row i that the rectangle's column interval is either entirely left of p(i) (i.e., max column <= p(i)-1) or entirely right of p(i) (i.e., min column >= p(i)+1). This is the condition for a rectangle to be fully in L or R. So the claim that each tile lies entirely in L or R is correct: any rectangle that includes cells from both sides of a hole in any row would cross the hole column in that row, covering it. But could a rectangle include cells left of the hole in some rows and cells right of the hole in other rows, but still avoid covering holes? Let's examine.\n\nSuppose rectangle's column interval is [c, d]. For each row i in the rectangle's row interval, we need either d <= p(i)-1 (i.e., rectangle is left of hole) or c >= p(i)+1 (i.e., rectangle is right of hole). If for some rows i we have d <= p(i)-1 and for other rows j we have c >= p(j)+1, then we need both conditions simultaneously for the same interval [c,d]. That would require d <= p(i)-1 for all rows i where rectangle is left, and c >= p(j)+1 for all rows j where rectangle is right. Since c <= d, we need c <= d. If we have any row i where p(i)-1 < c (i.e., hole column is left of c), then rectangle is right of that hole. If we have any row j where p(j)+1 > d (hole column is right of d), then rectangle is left of that hole. So for rectangle to be both left of some holes and right of others, we need c <= d and c >= p(j)+1 for some j and d <= p(i)-1 for some i. This is possible if p(j)+1 <= d <= p(i)-1, i.e., there is a gap between p(j) and p(i). But p(i) and p(j) are columns of holes in different rows. So if there exists a pair of rows where p(j) < p(i)-2 (i.e., p(i) - p(j) >= 2), then we could have rectangle column interval [p(j)+1, p(i)-1] which is between the two hole columns. That rectangle would be to the right of hole in row j (since its columns start at p(j)+1) and left of hole in row i (since its columns end at p(i)-1). So rectangle would avoid covering holes in both rows. However, does it intersect any other rows? For any other row k, we need to check that its hole column p(k) is either <= p(j) or >= p(i). If p(k) lies between p(j) and p(i), then rectangle's column interval would include p(k), covering that hole. So rectangle can be placed only if the set of rows included have holes all either <= p(j) or >= p(i). But if we choose rows where holes are all either to the left of a certain column or to the right of a certain column, then rectangle can be placed. In other words, rectangle can be placed in region between two hole columns, but only covering rows whose holes are all on one side of that region. But the solution's claim \"every tile lies entirely in L or entirely in R\" means that each tile must be either entirely left of all holes (i.e., in L) or entirely right of all holes (i.e., in R). But the above scenario suggests a tile could be placed in a vertical strip between two hole columns, covering rows where holes are all to the left or all to the right of that strip. However, the definition of L and R is per row: L = cells left of the hole in that row; R = cells right of the hole in that row. So a cell that is between two hole columns could be left of the hole in some rows and right of the hole in other rows. So such a cell would belong to L for some rows and to R for other rows. The solution's claim that each tile lies entirely in L or entirely in R would be false if a tile includes cells that are left of the hole in some rows and right of the hole in other rows. But does the tile's region necessarily include both L and R cells? Let's examine.\n\nDefine a tile as rectangle Q with row interval I = [a,b] and column interval [c,d]. For each row i in I, we have either d <= p(i)-1 (so Q is left of hole in row i) or c >= p(i)+1 (so Q is right of hole in row i). If for some rows i we have d <= p(i)-1 (i.e., Q is left of hole), and for some rows j we have c >= p(j)+1 (i.e., Q is right of hole), then Q includes cells that are left of the hole in some rows and right of the hole in others. So Q would contain cells that are in L for some rows and in R for others. The claim \"every tile lies entirely in L or entirely in R\" means that for each tile, either for all rows i in I we have d <= p(i)-1 (i.e., Q is left of all holes) or for all rows i we have c >= p(i)+1 (i.e., Q is right of all holes). The solution's proof of claim: \"suppose a rectangle Q contained a cell (i,j1) in L and a cell (i,j2) in R of the same row i. Since the column interval of Q is contiguous, it would also contain the column p(i) of the uncovered square (i,p(i)), contradicting requirement.\" This proof only considered the case where the rectangle contains cells from both L and R in the same row. However, the rectangle could contain cells from L in some rows and from R in other rows, without containing both sides in the same row. The proof does not address that scenario. So the claim is not fully proven. Is the claim actually false? Let's test with a concrete example.\n\nTake n=4, p = identity (holes at (1,1), (2,2), (3,3), (4,4)). L = cells where column < row (i>j). R = cells where column > row (i<j). Now consider a rectangle Q covering rows 1 and 2, columns 2 to 2 (i.e., column 2 only). Row 1: hole at column 1, so column 2 is to the right of hole => cell (1,2) is in R. Row 2: hole at column 2, cell (2,2) is the hole itself (uncovered). But we cannot cover the hole. So that rectangle is invalid because it would cover the hole. Let's try a rectangle covering rows 1 and 2, columns 1 to 1 (i.e., column 1). Row 1: hole at column 1, cell (1,1) is the hole, cannot cover. So not allowed.\n\nTry rectangle covering rows 1 and 2, columns 3 to 3 (col 3). Row 1: hole at col1, cell (1,3) is in R. Row 2: hole at col2, cell (2,3) is in R. So rectangle is entirely in R for both rows. So it's fine.\n\nNow try rectangle covering rows 1 and 3, columns 2 to 2 (col 2). Row 1: hole at col1, cell (1,2) is in R. Row 3: hole at col3, cell (3,2) is in L. So rectangle includes a cell in R for row 1 and a cell in L for row 3. Does it cover any hole? For row 1, column interval [2,2] does not include column 1 (hole). For row 3, column interval [2,2] does not include column 3 (hole). So rectangle does not cover any hole. It includes cells from both L and R but not in same row. So this rectangle is allowed under the problem constraints. However, does it lie entirely in L or entirely in R? No, because for row 1 it's in R, for row 3 it's in L. So the claim \"every tile lies entirely in L or entirely in R\" is false.\n\nThus the solution's claim is not valid. However, the lower bound argument uses this claim to argue that each tile lies entirely in L or R, and then they treat L and R separately, counting rectangles needed to cover each region. If the claim is false, the lower bound may be invalid because a rectangle could cover cells in both L and R (across rows) and thus reduce the total number of rectangles needed. So the lower bound of 2n-2 may not hold; perhaps we can cover L and R with fewer rectangles by using tiles that straddle both sides across rows. The solution's lower bound argument might be too strong.\n\nWe need to examine if the lower bound can be improved. The problem asks for the minimum number of tiles. The solution's answer is 4048. We need to verify if this is indeed minimal. Let's attempt to find a better tiling with fewer than 2n-2 = 4048 tiles. Perhaps we can tile the board with n tiles? Let's think.\n\nWe have n uncovered squares (one per row and column). The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix. We need to tile the rest. The uncovered squares are like holes. The board is essentially a grid with n holes, one per row and column. We need to tile the rest with rectangles. This is reminiscent of covering a board with rectangles avoiding a set of points. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover all cells except a permutation matrix is known to be 2n-2? I'm not sure. Let's try small n to test.\n\nWe can test for small n manually (though we cannot manually compute, but we can reason). For n=1, we have a 1x1 board, one uncovered square, no tiles needed, answer = 0. 2n-2 = 0, matches.\n\nFor n=2, board 2x2, need each row and column have exactly one uncovered square. So uncovered squares must be at positions (1,1) and (2,2) or (1,2) and (2,1). Let's consider identity (1,1) and (2,2). Then we have cells (1,2) and (2,1) to cover. They are not adjacent horizontally or vertically (they are at opposite corners). To cover them with rectangles (aligned to grid), each rectangle must be contiguous. We could cover each cell with a 1x1 tile. That's 2 tiles. Could we cover both with a single rectangle? The rectangle covering (1,2) and (2,1) would be the whole board (2x2) which includes holes (1,1) and (2,2), not allowed. Could we use a rectangle covering (1,2) and (2,2)? That includes hole (2,2). Not allowed. Similarly any rectangle covering both cells will include a hole. So we need at least 2 tiles. 2n-2 = 2*2-2 = 2. So matches.\n\nFor n=3, board 3x3, need uncovered squares form a permutation. Let's try identity (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). Then uncovered squares are diagonal. The remaining cells are all off-diagonal: 6 cells. The solution's construction uses vertical tiles for each column left of diagonal (columns 1,2) covering rows below diagonal, and vertical tiles for each column right of diagonal (columns 2,3) covering rows above diagonal. That yields 2n-2 = 4 tiles: left region: columns 1,2 vertical tiles (col1 rows 2-3, col2 row3). Right region: columns 2,3 vertical tiles (col2 rows1, col3 rows1-2). That's 4 tiles covering all off-diagonal cells. Is it possible to cover with fewer than 4 tiles? Let's try to find a tiling with 3 tiles. Could we use a rectangle that covers some left region cells and some right region cells across rows? For example, consider rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 2-3 (i.e., a 2x2 block at top right). This block includes cells (1,2), (1,3), (2,2), (2,3). However (2,2) is a hole (uncovered). So not allowed. How about rectangle covering rows 2-3, columns 1-2 (bottom left 2x2 block). That includes (2,2) hole. Not allowed. How about rectangle covering rows 1-3, columns 2-2 (vertical column 2). That includes (2,2) hole. Not allowed. So any rectangle covering column 2 will include (2,2) hole. Similarly column 1 includes (1,1) hole at row1, but we could restrict rows to 2-3, column1 only (vertical tile covering rows 2-3 col1) which is allowed (that's part of left region). Similarly column3 rows 1-2 allowed. So the only allowed rectangles are those that avoid the diagonal holes. The left region consists of cells (2,1), (3,1), (3,2). Right region cells (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). The solution uses vertical tiles: left region: col1 rows2-3 (covers (2,1),(3,1)), col2 row3 (covers (3,2)). Right region: col2 row1 (covers (1,2)), col3 rows1-2 (covers (1,3),(2,3)). That's 4 tiles. Could we combine (3,2) with (2,3) in a rectangle? They are not aligned: (3,2) is row3 col2, (2,3) is row2 col3. A rectangle covering rows2-3, columns2-3 would include (2,2) hole and (3,3) hole. Not allowed. Could we combine (1,2) and (3,2) in a rectangle? That would be column2 rows1-3, includes (2,2) hole. Not allowed. Could we combine (1,2) and (2,3) in a rectangle? That would be rows1-2, columns2-3 includes (2,2) hole. Not allowed. So seems we cannot reduce below 4. So for n=3, answer 4 matches 2n-2.\n\nFor n=4, identity holes at (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4). Off-diagonal cells: 12 cells. The solution's construction uses vertical tiles: left region columns 1-3 vertical covering rows below diagonal: col1 rows2-4 (3 cells), col2 rows3-4 (2 cells), col3 row4 (1 cell) => 3 tiles. Right region columns 2-4 vertical covering rows above diagonal: col2 row1 (1 cell), col3 rows1-2 (2 cells), col4 rows1-3 (3 cells) => 3 tiles. Total 6 tiles = 2n-2 = 6. Could we do fewer? Let's try to find a tiling with 5 tiles. Perhaps we can use a rectangle that covers some left region cells and some right region cells across rows, like a rectangle covering rows2-3, columns2-3 (2x2 block). That includes holes (2,2) and (3,3). Not allowed. What about rectangle covering rows1-4, columns2-2 (col2 all rows). That includes hole (2,2). Not allowed. Similarly any rectangle covering column i will include hole (i,i). So any rectangle that includes column i must avoid row i. So we could have a rectangle covering column i but only rows not equal to i. That's allowed: e.g., column2 rows1,3,4 (noncontiguous rows) cannot be a rectangle because rows must be contiguous. So we could have rectangle covering rows1-2, column2 (rows1-2). That includes hole (2,2). Not allowed. So column2 can only be covered by vertical tile covering rows3-4 (col2 rows3-4) which is allowed (since rows3-4 are contiguous and do not include row2). That's exactly the left region tile for col2 row3-4? Wait left region tile for col2 includes rows3-4 (since left region includes cells left of diagonal: for col2, rows >2 are left region cells (3,2) and (4,2). So that tile is allowed. Similarly column3 can be covered by rows4 only (col3 row4) left region, and rows1-2 (col3 rows1-2) right region. So we need separate tiles for column3 above and below diagonal. So the solution's tiling seems optimal.\n\nBut maybe there is a more clever tiling that uses horizontal tiles instead of vertical, or uses larger rectangles that combine multiple columns but avoid holes by skipping rows. For instance, consider rectangle covering rows3-4, columns2-3 (2x2 block). That includes cells (3,2),(3,3),(4,2),(4,3). However (3,3) is a hole, so not allowed. Similarly any rectangle that includes a column i and row i will include hole (i,i). So to avoid holes, any rectangle must avoid including any cell (i,i). So the rectangle's row interval and column interval must not intersect at any point where row = column (i.e., diagonal). So rectangle must be entirely above diagonal (i.e., all rows < columns) or entirely below diagonal (all rows > columns), or must be such that the row interval and column interval do not intersect at any i. That is, either max row < min column (rectangle entirely above diagonal) or min row > max column (rectangle entirely below diagonal). Indeed, if we have a rectangle with row interval [a,b] and column interval [c,d], then for any i in [a,b] and any j in [c,d], the cell (i,j) is included. If there exists i=j in the intersection of intervals, then the rectangle includes a hole. So to avoid holes, we need [a,b] and [c,d] to be disjoint as intervals (no common integer). That is, either b < c (rows entirely above columns) or d < a (columns entirely left of rows). In other words, the rectangle must lie entirely in the region strictly above the diagonal (R) or strictly below (L). This matches the solution's claim that each tile lies entirely in L or R. Wait, earlier we found a counterexample: rectangle covering rows 1 and 3, column 2 only (col2 rows1 and 3). Row interval [1,3] includes rows 1,2,3 (since rectangle must include contiguous rows). Actually a rectangle covering rows 1 and 3 but not row 2 is not possible because rows must be contiguous. So our earlier example of rectangle covering rows 1 and 3, column 2 only is not a rectangle because rows 1-3 includes row 2, and column 2 only includes column 2. So the rectangle would include row 2 as well, covering cell (2,2) which is a hole. So it's not allowed. Indeed, a rectangle's row interval must be contiguous, thus includes all rows between min and max. So any rectangle that includes rows i and j must also include all rows between them. So the earlier counterexample fails because we cannot have a rectangle that includes row 1 and row 3 but not row 2. So the claim that each tile lies entirely in L or R might be correct after all, because any rectangle that includes cells from both L and R must have rows that include a row where the hole column lies between the column interval, causing a hole. Let's analyze more formally.\n\nGiven a rectangle Q with row interval I = [a,b] and column interval J = [c,d]. For each row i in I, the rectangle includes cells (i, j) for all j in J. For Q to avoid covering the hole at (i, p(i)), we need p(i) not in J for any i in I. So for each i in I, either p(i) < c (hole left of rectangle) or p(i) > d (hole right of rectangle). So for each i, p(i) is either less than c or greater than d. If there exist rows i and i' in I such that p(i) < c and p(i') > d, then we have p(i) < c <= d < p(i'), so intervals [c,d] and [p(i), p(i)] are disjoint. But that's okay; the rectangle doesn't include those holes. However, we need to check if there is any row i in I where p(i) lies between c and d (inclusive). If any such row exists, rectangle covers a hole, not allowed. So the condition is that for all i in I, p(i) is either < c or > d. So the set of p(i) values for rows in I must be either all less than c or all greater than d. That is, either max_{i in I} p(i) < c (all holes left of rectangle) or min_{i in I} p(i) > d (all holes right of rectangle). If there is a mix (some holes left, some holes right), then we have both p(i) < c for some i and p(j) > d for some j. Then we have max left holes < c <= d < min right holes? Actually we need to check if there is any row where p(i) is between c and d. If we have some holes left of rectangle (p(i) < c) and some holes right of rectangle (p(j) > d), then there is a gap: all holes left are < c, all holes right are > d. Since c <= d, there is no hole between c and d. So the condition holds: for all rows i, p(i) is either < c or > d. So the rectangle does not intersect any hole. However, is this possible? Let's consider a permutation p. For a given interval I = [a,b], the values p(i) for i in I are some set of integers. If the set of p(i) values is not contiguous, it's possible that they all lie either below c or above d, with a gap in between. For example, suppose p(i) values for rows 1-3 are {1,3,5}. Choose rectangle with column interval [2,4] (c=2,d=4). Then p(1)=1<2, p(2)=3 is between 2 and 4 (so hole at row2 column3 would be covered). So not allowed. But if p(i) values are {1,5,6} and rectangle column interval [2,4], then p(1)=1<2, p(2)=5>4, p(3)=6>4. So all holes are either left or right of rectangle, none in between. So rectangle does not cover any hole. So it's allowed. However, does such a rectangle exist? Let's test with n=5, p identity (1,2,3,4,5). For rows 1-3, p(i) = {1,2,3}. If we choose column interval [4,5], then all holes are left of rectangle (max p(i) = 3 < 4). So rectangle is right of all holes for those rows. That's allowed. That's a rectangle entirely in R (right region). Similarly, if we choose column interval [1,1] for rows 2-5, p(i) values are {2,3,4,5} all > 1, so rectangle is left of all holes for those rows (i.e., in L). That's allowed. So these are just L or R rectangles.\n\nBut the scenario where some rows have holes left and some rows have holes right relative to the rectangle's column interval is possible only if the set of p(i) values for rows I is split across the interval [c,d] with a gap. For example, suppose p(i) values for rows I are {1,5}. Choose rectangle column interval [2,4]. Then p(i) = 1 < 2 (left) and p(j) = 5 > 4 (right). So rectangle's column interval [2,4] is between the holes. Does this rectangle intersect any hole? No, because holes are at columns 1 and 5, not in [2,4]. So rectangle does not cover any hole. However, does the rectangle lie entirely in L or R? For row i where p(i) = 1, rectangle's columns [2,4] are to the right of hole, so that row's cells are in R. For row j where p(j) = 5, rectangle's columns [2,4] are to the left of hole, so that row's cells are in L. So rectangle includes cells that are in R for some rows and L for others. So the rectangle is not entirely in L or entirely in R. So the claim \"every tile lies entirely in L or entirely in R\" is false if such a rectangle can exist.\n\nNow, can such a rectangle exist given the constraints that the rectangle's rows must be contiguous and the set of holes (p(i)) for those rows must have a gap that allows a column interval between them? Yes, if the permutation p has a \"jump\" such that for some contiguous block of rows, the set of hole columns is not contiguous. For identity permutation, p(i) = i, the set of hole columns for any contiguous block of rows is also contiguous (since it's a set of consecutive integers). For identity, any contiguous block of rows [a,b] yields p(i) values {a, a+1, ..., b}, which is contiguous. So there is no gap; any column interval that lies between min and max will intersect some hole. Therefore for identity permutation, any rectangle must be entirely left or right of all holes for that block, i.e., must be entirely in L or R. So the claim holds for identity permutation. However, the lower bound argument must hold for any permutation p, because we don't know which permutation yields minimal number of tiles. The solution's lower bound argument uses the claim for any p. If the claim fails for some p, the lower bound might be weaker (i.e., we might be able to tile with fewer rectangles using a permutation that has gaps). But the solution also uses a construction for identity permutation achieving 2n-2 tiles. So the minimal number of tiles could be less than 2n-2 if there exists a permutation that allows a tiling with fewer tiles. The solution's lower bound must hold for all permutations. If the claim fails for some permutation, the lower bound might not hold for that permutation, but maybe the minimal number of tiles across all permutations is still 2n-2 because any permutation that allows fewer tiles would require some other constraints that increase number of tiles elsewhere. We need to examine if there exists a permutation that allows tiling with fewer than 2n-2 rectangles.\n\nWe need to think about the problem: We can choose any arrangement of uncovered squares (permutation). We want to minimize the number of tiles needed to cover the rest. So we can choose the permutation that yields the easiest covering. The solution picks identity and shows a tiling with 2n-2 tiles. But maybe there is a better permutation that yields a covering with fewer tiles. The lower bound argument tries to show that any covering must use at least 2n-2 tiles, regardless of permutation. However, the claim that each tile lies entirely in L or R is used to separate the covering into left and right regions, each requiring at least n-1 tiles. The lower bound for each region uses a counting argument based on c_i cells (rightmost left cell) and d_i cells (leftmost right cell). The argument that any rectangle in L can contain at most one c_i cell relies on the property that for any rectangle in L, the column interval's rightmost column r is less than or equal to p(i)-1 for all rows i in the rectangle. Then they argue that the rectangle must contain the cell c_i for the row where p(i) is minimal. That seems correct. But the crucial assumption is that any rectangle in L cannot contain more than one c_i. However, if a rectangle is not entirely in L (i.e., includes rows where it's in R), then the argument fails. But they restrict to rectangles in L. So the lower bound for L is based on the assumption that any rectangle covering left region cells must be entirely in L. But we just argued that a rectangle could be partially left and partially right across rows, but still avoid holes. However, such a rectangle would not be entirely in L, but would still cover some left region cells. So the lower bound for L that counts rectangles covering L may be invalid because some rectangles covering left region cells might also cover right region cells, thus reducing the number of rectangles needed for L alone. However, the lower bound argument counts rectangles needed to cover L, but if some rectangles also cover R, they still count as covering L. So the lower bound for L might be lower than n-1 if we allow rectangles that also cover R. But the lower bound argument uses the property that any rectangle in L can contain at most one c_i. If we allow rectangles that also cover R, they might contain multiple c_i cells because they might include rows where the rectangle is in R for those rows, but the rectangle may still contain left region cells for some rows. Could such a rectangle contain two c_i cells? Let's examine.\n\nSuppose we have a rectangle Q with row interval I = [a,b] and column interval J = [c,d]. For each row i in I, the rectangle includes cells (i,j) for all j in J. For a row i where p(i) > d (hole to the right), the rectangle's cells are left of hole, i.e., in L. For a row i where p(i) < c (hole to the left), the rectangle's cells are right of hole, i.e., in R. For rows where p(i) is between c and d, the rectangle would cover a hole, not allowed. So for Q to be allowed, the set of rows I must be partitioned into two subsets: I_left = {i in I: p(i) > d} (holes to the right) and I_right = {i in I: p(i) < c} (holes to the left). For rows in I_left, rectangle's cells are in L; for rows in I_right, rectangle's cells are in R. So Q can contain both L and R cells, but not holes. Now, consider the c_i cells: c_i = (i, p(i)-1) for rows where p(i) > 1. For a row i in I_left, p(i) > d >= c (since c <= d). Then p(i)-1 >= d >= c. Actually p(i)-1 >= d? Let's check: p(i) > d, so p(i)-1 >= d (since p(i) integer). So c_i column is >= d. But the rectangle's column interval is [c,d]; c_i column = p(i)-1 >= d, could be equal to d or greater. If p(i)-1 = d, then c_i is at column d, which is the rightmost column of rectangle, so c_i is in Q. If p(i)-1 > d, then c_i is to the right of rectangle, not in Q. So for rows in I_left, rectangle may contain the c_i cell only if p(i)-1 = d, i.e., p(i) = d+1. So rectangle can contain at most one c_i cell for each row where p(i) = d+1. But there could be multiple rows with p(i) = d+1? No, p is a permutation, so each column appears exactly once, so there is at most one row with p(i) = d+1. So rectangle can contain at most one c_i cell from I_left.\n\nNow consider rows in I_right (holes left of rectangle). For those rows, p(i) < c. Then p(i)-1 < c-1 <= d-1 (since c <= d). So c_i column is < c, thus left of rectangle's column interval, not in Q. So rectangle cannot contain any c_i from rows in I_right. So overall, rectangle can contain at most one c_i cell (the one where p(i) = d+1). So the argument that any rectangle can contain at most one c_i holds even for rectangles that cross between L and R, as long as they avoid holes. Actually the argument used the minimal p(i) in I to identify c_i = (i, p(i)-1) = (i,r) where r = min_{i in I} p(i)-1. In the scenario where the rectangle's column interval is [c,d], we have r = d (the rightmost column). So the row i0 where p(i) is minimal among rows in I (i.e., p(i0) = min_{i in I} p(i)) yields c_i0 = (i0, p(i0)-1) = (i0, r). So rectangle contains c_i0. For any other row i in I, p(i) > p(i0) => p(i)-1 > r = d, so c_i is not in rectangle. So indeed any rectangle can contain at most one c_i, regardless of whether rectangle includes both L and R cells. So the lower bound argument for L might still hold: covering all c_i cells (n-1 of them) requires at least n-1 rectangles, because each rectangle can contain at most one c_i. However, note that c_i cells are defined only for rows where p(i)>1. For rows where p(i)=1, c_i does not exist. So there are n-1 c_i cells. The argument shows that any rectangle (not necessarily wholly in L) can contain at most one c_i. But does it require that each rectangle covering L must contain at least one c_i? No, a rectangle could be placed entirely in R (i.e., covering only right region cells) and not contain any c_i. So the lower bound argument that covering all of L requires at least n-1 rectangles assumes that each rectangle covering L must contain at least one c_i. But if a rectangle covers some L cells but also covers some R cells, it might not contain any c_i? Let's examine: Suppose rectangle Q includes some left region cells. For a row i where Q includes left region cells, we have p(i) > d (hole to right). Then c_i = (i, p(i)-1). As argued, c_i is in Q only if p(i)-1 = d (i.e., p(i) = d+1). If p(i) > d+1, then c_i is to the right of rectangle, not in Q. So Q could include left region cells for a row i where p(i) > d+1, but not include c_i. So a rectangle could cover left region cells without covering any c_i. However, the lower bound argument uses the fact that each rectangle can contain at most one c_i, but does not claim that each rectangle must contain a c_i. The argument says: \"Since the c_i are all distinct, covering all of L requires at least n-1 rectangles.\" This implicitly assumes that each rectangle covering L must contain at least one c_i. But is that true? Let's examine: Suppose we have a rectangle that covers left region cells but does not contain any c_i. Then that rectangle would not be counted towards covering c_i cells, but it would still cover some left region cells. However, the lower bound argument is based on covering all c_i cells, not covering all left region cells. The argument is: there are n-1 distinct c_i cells. Any rectangle can contain at most one c_i. Therefore, to cover all c_i cells, we need at least n-1 rectangles. But covering all c_i cells does not guarantee covering all left region cells. However, we need to cover all left region cells, not just c_i. So covering all c_i cells is necessary but not sufficient. The lower bound argument says that covering L requires at least n-1 rectangles because you need to cover the n-1 c_i cells, each rectangle can cover at most one. That is a valid lower bound: any covering of L must also cover all c_i cells (since c_i are in L). So at least n-1 rectangles are needed. Even if some rectangles also cover R cells, they still can cover at most one c_i each, so you need at least n-1 rectangles to cover all c_i. So the lower bound for L holds regardless of whether rectangles also cover R. So the claim that each rectangle lies entirely in L or R is not needed for the lower bound; the lower bound uses c_i cells which are in L. As long as any rectangle can contain at most one c_i, the lower bound holds. The argument that any rectangle can contain at most one c_i is valid for any rectangle that does not cover any hole. We need to verify that the argument holds for any rectangle that does not intersect any hole. The proof given: For any rectangle Q, define row interval I = [a,b] and column interval [ℓ, r]. Since Q ⊆ L, they assume Q is in L. But we need to check if the argument that any rectangle can contain at most one c_i holds even if Q is not entirely in L. The solution's argument for L uses the assumption Q ⊆ L to derive r ≤ p(i)-1 for all i in I. If Q is not entirely in L, then for some rows i,