\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this gives \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let the unique uncovered unit square be at column \\(\\sigma(i)\\), where \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad R_i=n-\\sigma(i),\n\\]\n\nthe numbers of squares to the left and to the right of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).  \nClearly \\(L_i+R_i=n-1\\) for every \\(i\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1. Lower bound\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le L_i\\},\n\\]\n\nthe squares lying strictly left of the uncovered squares.  \nBecause the \\(\\sigma(i)\\) are all distinct, the values \\(L_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\) form the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nHence the multiset \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) contains each integer from \\(0\\) to \\(n-1\\) exactly once; in particular there are \\(n-1\\) distinct positive values among the \\(L_i\\).\n\nThe region \\(\\mathcal L\\) is a Young diagram: each row \\(i\\) consists of the first \\(L_i\\) squares of that row, and the row lengths are non‑increasing when the rows are reordered by decreasing \\(L_i\\).  \n\nA rectangle used in a tiling of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must have a constant width across all rows it occupies; consequently a rectangle can only cover rows whose lengths are at least that width.  \nIf two rows have different positive lengths, a single rectangle cannot finish both rows, because after covering the shorter row the longer row would still have uncovered squares to its right.  \nThus at least one rectangle is required for each distinct positive row length.  \nSince there are \\(n-1\\) distinct positive lengths, any tiling of \\(\\mathcal L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nAn analogous argument applied to the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe squares strictly right of the uncovered squares, shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to tile \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\nA rectangle cannot contain squares from both \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(\\mathcal R\\): if a rectangle contained a square \\((i,j_1)\\in\\mathcal L\\) and a square \\((i,j_2)\\in\\mathcal R\\) in the same row \\(i\\), then its column interval would contain the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) of the uncovered square, forcing the rectangle to cover that uncovered square, which is forbidden.  \nHence the tilings of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(\\mathcal R\\) are independent, and the total number of tiles is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Upper bound – explicit construction\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e., the uncovered squares are the main diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).\n\n**Tiles covering \\(\\mathcal L\\).**  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{vert}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{\\text{vert}}_j\\) has dimensions \\((n-j)\\times1\\) and covers precisely the squares of column \\(j\\) that lie below the diagonal, i.e., the squares of \\(\\mathcal L\\) in that column.  \nThe collection \\(\\{T^{\\text{vert}}_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles and covers all of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n**Tiles covering \\(\\mathcal R\\).**  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{horiz}}_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{\\text{horiz}}_i\\) has dimensions \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) and covers precisely the squares of row \\(i\\) that lie to the right of the diagonal, i.e., the squares of \\(\\mathcal R\\) in that row.  \nThe collection \\(\\{T^{\\text{horiz}}_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles and covers all of \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\nThe two families of rectangles are disjoint because a vertical rectangle lies entirely in a column left of the diagonal while a horizontal rectangle lies entirely in a row above the diagonal; their interiors never intersect.  \nAll unit squares not on the diagonal are covered exactly once, and each diagonal square \\((i,i)\\) remains uncovered.  \nThus we have a tiling using exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound shows that any admissible tiling requires at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above achieves this number.  \nConsequently the minimum possible number of tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board with one uncovered square per row and column is \\(2n-2\\).  \n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
