ft‑anchored rectangles  \n\nFix the permutation \\(p\\) and write \\(\\ell_i=p(i)-1\\) (\\(0\\le\\ell_i\\le n-1\\)).  \nFor each column index \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the binary word\n\\[\nB_k(i)=\\begin{cases}\n1,&\\ell_i\\ge k\\;( \\text{i.e. }p(i)>k),\\\\[2pt]\n0,&\\ell_i<k\\;( \\text{i.e. }p(i)\\le k).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe set of rows that contain a cell of column \\(k\\) in the left region is precisely\n\\[\nS_k:=\\{\\,i\\mid B_k(i)=1\\,\\}.\n\\]\nThe rows of \\(S_k\\) split into a certain number \\(\\operatorname{comp}(B_k)\\) of maximal contiguous intervals; each such interval forces a distinct left‑anchored rectangle covering column \\(k\\). Consequently the **minimal** number of left‑anchored rectangles needed to cover \\(L\\) equals\n\\[\nL_{\\min}= \\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\operatorname{comp}(B_k).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor a binary word \\(B\\) of length \\(n\\) we have the elementary identity\n\\[\n\\operatorname{comp}(B)=1+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}[B(i)\\neq B(i+1)],\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nwhere \\([\\,\\cdot\\,]\\) denotes the Iverson bracket (value \\(1\\) if the condition holds, \\(0\\) otherwise).  \nIndeed, each change between consecutive symbols creates a new interval, and there is always one initial interval.\n\nInsert (5) into (4):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{\\min}\n   &=\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\Bigl(1+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}[B_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)]\\Bigr)\\\\\n   &=(n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}[B_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)] .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFix an adjacent pair of rows \\((i,i+1)\\).  \nFrom the definition (3) we have \\(B_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)\\) exactly when \\(k\\) lies strictly between the two values \\(p(i)\\) and \\(p(i+1)\\):\n\\[\nB_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)\\iff\n\\min\\{p(i),p(i+1)\\}\\le k<\\max\\{p(i),p(i+1)\\}.\n\\]\nHence the inner sum in (6) counts precisely\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1,\\;B_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)\\}\\bigr|\n   =|p(i)-p(i+1)|.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (7) into (6) yields\n\\[\nL_{\\min}= (n-1)+\\sum_