".\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares:**  \n   - Each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, so the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there is a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (with \\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\n2. **Decomposition into left\u2011 and right\u2011anchored rectangles:**  \n   - For a fixed permutation \\(p\\), the cells left of the holes are  \n     \\[\n       L=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\n     \\]\n     and the cells right of the holes are  \n     \\[\n       R=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n     \\]\n   - Any axis\u2011parallel rectangle covering cells of \\(L\\) (resp. \\(R\\)) must be anchored at the left (resp. right) border of the board; otherwise it would contain a hole.  \n   - Hence the whole tiling consists of a family of left\u2011anchored rectangles covering \\(L\\) and a family of right\u2011anchored rectangles covering \\(R\\).\n\n3. **Counting the minimal number of anchored rectangles:**  \n   - For each column \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le n-1)\\) define the binary word  \n     \\[\n       B_k(i)=\\begin{cases}\n                1,&p(i)>k,\\\\[2pt]\n                0,&p(i)\\le k .\n              \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n     The rows where column \\(k\\) belongs to \\(L\\) are exactly the positions where \\(B_k(i)=1\\).  \n   - The rows with \\(B_k(i)=1\\) split into a certain number \\(\\operatorname{comp}(B_k)\\) of contiguous intervals; each such interval forces a distinct left\u2011anchored rectangle covering column \\(k\\).  \n   - Consequently the minimal number of left\u2011anchored rectangles equals  \n     \\[\n       \\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\operatorname{comp}(B_k).\n     \\]\n   - An analogous expression holds for the right side, using the words \\(C_k(i)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{p(i)\\le k\\}}\\).  \n   - For any binary word, \\(\\operatorname{comp}(B_k)=1+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}[B_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)]\\). Summing over all \\(k\\) gives\n     \\[\n       \\begin{aligned}\n         \\text{total rectangles}\n         &=\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\Bigl(1+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}[B_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)]\\Bigr)\\\\\n         &=(n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}[B_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)] .\n       \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n   - For a fixed adjacent pair \\((i,i+1)\\) the inner sum counts the integers \\(k\\) that lie strictly between \\(p(i)\\) and \\(p(i+1)\\); therefore\n     \\[\n       \\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}[B_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)]=|p(i)-p(i+1)|.\n     \\]\n   - Hence for any permutation \\(p\\)\n     \\[\n       \\boxed{\\,T(p)= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|p(i)-p(i+1)|\\,}\n     \\]\n     is the minimal possible number of tiles.\n\n4. **Optimising over all permutations:**  \n   - Every term \\(|p(i)-p(i+1)|\\) is at least \\(1\\); therefore\n     \\[\n       \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|p(i)-p(i+1)|\\ge n-1 .\n     \\]\n   - Equality holds precisely for the monotone permutations \\(p(i)=i\\) or \\(p(i)=n+1-i\\).  \n   - For these permutations \\(T(p)= (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\\).\n\n5. **Explicit construction attaining the bound:**  \n   - Choose the monotone permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n   - For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a left\u2011anchored rectangle covering rows \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\) and columns \\(1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n   - For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a right\u2011anchored rectangle covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,n-k\\) and columns \\(n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   - These \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, cover every cell except the diagonal, and each row and column contains exactly one uncovered square.  \n\n6. **Conclusion:**  \n   - The minimal number of tiles required for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) board is  \n     \\[\n       2(2025-1)=4048 .\n     \\]\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work with a general \\(n\\times n\\) board; the final answer will be obtained by substituting \\(n=2025\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there is a bijection  \n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nThus the set of uncovered cells is\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   Left and right regions  \n\nFor a row \\(i\\) the cells **left** of the hole are\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\},\n\\qquad\n\\ell_i:=|L_i|=p(i)-1 .\n\\]\nThe cells **right** of the hole are\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\},\n\\qquad\nr_i:=|R_i|=n-p(i) .\n\\]\n\nAny axis\u2011parallel rectangle that contains a cell of \\(L_i\\) cannot contain the hole \\((i,p(i))\\); consequently its whole column interval must lie in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,p(i)-1\\}\\). Hence every rectangle covering cells of the set\n\\[\nL:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\n\\]\nis **left\u2011anchored**: it has the form\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in I,\\;1\\le j\\le w\\}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nfor some non\u2011empty interval of rows \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and some integer \\(w\\ge1\\) satisfying \\(w\\le\\ell_i\\) for all \\(i\\in I\\).  \nAnalogously, every rectangle covering cells of\n\\[\nR:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i\n\\]\nis **right\u2011anchored** and has the form\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in I,\\;n-w+1\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nwith \\(w\\le r_i\\) for all \\(i\\in I\\).\n\nThus any admissible tiling of the board consists of a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of left\u2011anchored rectangles covering \\(L\\) and a family \\(\\mathcal R\\) of right\u2011anchored rectangles covering \\(R\\); the two families are disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   Minimal number of left\u2011anchored rectangles  \n\nFix the permutation \\(p\\) and write \\(\\ell_i=p(i)-1\\) (\\(0\\le\\ell_i\\le n-1\\)).  \nFor each column index \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the binary word\n\\[\nB_k(i)=\\begin{cases}\n1,&\\ell_i\\ge k\\;( \\text{i.e. }p(i)>k),\\\\[2pt]\n0,&\\ell_i<k\\;( \\text{i.e. }p(i)\\le k).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe set of rows that contain a cell of column \\(k\\) in the left region is precisely\n\\[\nS_k:=\\{\\,i\\mid B_k(i)=1\\,\\}.\n\\]\nThe rows of \\(S_k\\) split into a certain number \\(\\operatorname{comp}(B_k)\\) of maximal contiguous intervals; each such interval forces a distinct left\u2011anchored rectangle covering column \\(k\\). Consequently the **minimal** number of left\u2011anchored rectangles needed to cover \\(L\\) equals\n\\[\nL_{\\min}= \\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\operatorname{comp}(B_k).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor a binary word \\(B\\) of length \\(n\\) we have the elementary identity\n\\[\n\\operatorname{comp}(B)=1+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}[B(i)\\neq B(i+1)],\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nwhere \\([\\,\\cdot\\,]\\) denotes the Iverson bracket (value \\(1\\) if the condition holds, \\(0\\) otherwise).  \nIndeed, each change between consecutive symbols creates a new interval, and there is always one initial interval.\n\nInsert (5) into (4):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{\\min}\n   &=\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\Bigl(1+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}[B_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)]\\Bigr)\\\\\n   &=(n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}[B_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)] .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFix an adjacent pair of rows \\((i,i+1)\\).  \nFrom the definition (3) we have \\(B_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)\\) exactly when \\(k\\) lies strictly between the two values \\(p(i)\\) and \\(p(i+1)\\):\n\\[\nB_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)\\iff\n\\min\\{p(i),p(i+1)\\}\\le k<\\max\\{p(i),p(i+1)\\}.\n\\]\nHence the inner sum in (6) counts precisely\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1,\\;B_k(i)\\neq B_k(i+1)\\}\\bigr|\n   =|p(i)-p(i+1)|.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (7) into (6) yields\n\\[\nL_{\\min}= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|p(i)-p(i+1)|.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Right\u2011anchored rectangles  \n\nThe right side is symmetric.  \nDefine \\(C_k(i)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{p(i)\\le k\\}}\\) for \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nExactly the same reasoning as above gives that the minimal number of right\u2011anchored rectangles equals the same expression (8).  \n\nTherefore the **total** minimal number of tiles required for the permutation \\(p\\) is\n\\[\n\\boxed{T(p)= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|p(i)-p(i+1)| } .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   Optimising over all permutations  \n\nEach summand \\(|p(i)-p(i+1)|\\) is a positive integer, at least \\(1\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|p(i)-p(i+1)|\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (10) holds exactly when every consecutive pair differs by \\(1\\); that is, when the permutation is monotone:\n\\[\np(i)=i\\quad\\text{or}\\quad p(i)=n+1-i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFor such a permutation the right\u2011hand side of (9) becomes\n\\[\nT(p)= (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus **no tiling can use fewer than \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles**, and the bound \\(2(n-1)\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.   Explicit tiling attaining the bound  \n\nTake the monotone permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the left\u2011anchored rectangle\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells left of the diagonal.\n\nSimilarly, for each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the right\u2011anchored rectangle\n\\[\n\\mathcal R_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-k,\\;n-k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint from each other and from the \\(\\mathcal L_k\\), and they cover all cells right of the diagonal.\n\nEvery row \\(i\\) is completely covered except the cell \\((i,i)\\); likewise every column \\(j\\) is completely covered except the cell \\((j,j)\\). Hence the construction satisfies the problem\u2019s requirements and uses exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.   Final answer  \n\nFor the original board \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2(2025-1)=\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
