 (the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\)).  \n\n   - For each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a horizontal rectangle covering the left side of row \\(i\\):  \n\n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n   - For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal rectangle covering the right side of row \\(i\\):  \n\n     \\[\n     K_i=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n     \\]\n\n   The \\(H_i\\)’s are \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering all cells left of the diagonal; the \\(K_i\\)’s are another \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering all cells right of the diagonal. No rectangle contains a diagonal cell, and every non‑diagonal cell is covered exactly once. Hence we obtain a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(p(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Decomposition of the covered squares  \n\nA unit square \\((i,j)\\) is covered iff \\(j\\neq p(i)\\).  \nHence the set of covered squares is the disjoint union  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA rectangle tile cannot contain an uncovered square, therefore every tile is\ncontained wholly in \\(L\\) or wholly in \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the left part \\(L\\)\n\nFor row \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=p(i)-1\\qquad (0\\le\\ell_i\\le n-1)\n\\]\n\nbe the number of covered cells of that row lying in \\(L\\).  \nSince \\(p\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus exactly \\(n-1\\) rows have \\(\\ell_i>0\\), and those positive lengths are all\ndifferent.\n\nThe region \\(L\\) is a **histogram**: in row \\(i\\) the covered cells form the\ncontiguous block  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le\\ell_i\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma (Histogram with distinct heights)  \n\nLet a histogram consist of \\(m\\) rows, each row \\(i\\) containing a block of\n\\(\\ell_i>0\\) cells starting at column 1, and assume the positive numbers\n\\(\\ell_i\\) are pairwise distinct.  \nAny partition of this histogram into axis‑aligned rectangles uses at least\n\\(m\\) rectangles (one for each row with \\(\\ell_i>0\\)).\n\n**Proof.**  \nProceed by induction on the number \\(m\\) of rows having \\(\\ell_i>0\\).\n\n*Base \\(m=1\\).*  The single non‑empty row must be covered by a rectangle,\nso at least one rectangle is needed.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Let \\(\\ell_{i_0}\\) be the maximal positive length; it is\nunique because the lengths are distinct.  \nConsider the cell \\((i_0,\\ell_{i_0})\\), the rightmost cell of that row.\nAny rectangle containing this cell must have its rightmost column \\(\\le\n\\ell_{i_0}\\); otherwise it would contain column \\(\\ell_{i_0}+1\\), which does\nnot belong to row \\(i_0\\).  Moreover, if the rectangle also contained a\ndifferent row \\(i\\) with \\(\\ell_i<\\ell_{i_0}\\), then column\n\\(\\ell_{i_0}\\) would lie outside the block of row \\(i\\), contradicting the\nrectangular shape.  Consequently the only possible rectangle covering\n\\((i_0,\\ell_{i_0})\\) is the horizontal strip  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i_0,j):1\\le j\\le\\ell_{i_0}\\}.\n\\]\n\nRemoving this strip deletes row \\(i_0\\) from the histogram, leaving a\nhistogram with \\(m-1\\) rows whose positive lengths are still distinct.\nBy the induction hypothesis at least \\(m-1\\) further rectangles are needed.\nHence the original histogram needs at least \\(1+(m-1)=m\\) rectangles. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nApplying the lemma to \\(L\\) we obtain:\n\n*Since exactly \\(n-1\\) rows have \\(\\ell_i>0\\) and the \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct,\nany partition of \\(L\\) into rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The right part \\(R\\)\n\nDefine for each row  \n\n\\[\nr_i:=n-p(i)\\qquad (0\\le r_i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(R\\) consists of the right‑hand blocks  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j):n-r_i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nand the multiset \\(\\{r_i\\}\\) also equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) (because\n\\(r_i=n-p(i)\\) and \\(p\\) is a permutation).  Thus exactly \\(n-1\\) rows have\n\\(r_i>0\\) and those positive lengths are pairwise distinct.  By the same\nlemma, any partition of \\(R\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Global lower bound\n\nSince every rectangle lies wholly in \\(L\\) or wholly in \\(R\\),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad(1\\le i\\le n),\n\\]\n\nso the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left side.*  \nFor each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells\nwith \\(j<i\\) (the whole set \\(L\\)).\n\n*Right side.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nK_i=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint from each other