"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   Let \\(n=2025\\).  \n   In each row \\(i\\) denote by \\(p(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square.  \n   Because each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.\n\n2. **Decomposition into left and right parts.**  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   L=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \n   R=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n   \\]  \n   Any rectangular tile cannot contain an uncovered square; consequently every tile is contained entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).  \n   Hence the total number of tiles equals the number of tiles covering \\(L\\) plus the number covering \\(R\\).\n\n3. **Lower bound for a side (Lemma).**  \n   For each row \\(i\\) let \\(\\ell_i=p(i)-1\\) be the length of its left block.  \n   Because \\(p\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); thus exactly \\(n-1\\) rows have \\(\\ell_i>0\\) and those positive lengths are pairwise distinct.  \n\n   *Lemma.* In a left\u2011aligned region whose rows have distinct positive lengths, any partition into axis\u2011aligned rectangles uses at least one rectangle for each row with positive length.  \n\n   *Proof of Lemma.*  \n   Consider the set  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(i,\\ell_i):\\ell_i>0\\},\n   \\]  \n   i.e. the rightmost cell of each non\u2011empty left block.  \n   If a rectangle \\(T\\subseteq L\\) contained two distinct cells \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) and \\((k,\\ell_k)\\) with \\(\\ell_i\\neq\\ell_k\\), assume \\(\\ell_i>\\ell_k\\).  \n   Then the column interval of \\(T\\) contains both \\(\\ell_k\\) and \\(\\ell_i\\); consequently it contains the cell \\((k,\\ell_i)\\).  \n   Since \\(\\ell_i>\\ell_k\\), the cell \\((k,\\ell_i)\\) does **not** belong to \\(L\\), contradicting \\(T\\subseteq L\\).  \n   Hence each rectangle of \\(L\\) can contain at most one element of \\(S\\).  \n   Because \\(|S|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(L\\). \u220e\n\n   Applying the lemma to \\(L\\) yields a lower bound of \\(n-1\\) tiles for the left side.  \n   The same argument applied to the right side (with lengths \\(r_i=n-p(i)\\)) gives another lower bound of \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n   Therefore any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]  \n\n   tiles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n   - For each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n     \\]  \n     covering the left block of row \\(i\\).  \n\n   - For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n     \\[\n     K_i=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n     \\]  \n     covering the right block of row \\(i\\).  \n\n   The \\(H_i\\)\u2019s are \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering all cells of \\(L\\); the \\(K_i\\)\u2019s are \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering all cells of \\(R\\).  \n   No rectangle contains a diagonal cell, and every non\u2011diagonal cell belongs to exactly one rectangle.  \n   Hence this construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(p(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Decomposition of the covered region  \n\nA unit square \\((i,j)\\) is covered iff \\(j\\neq p(i)\\).  \nHence the set of covered squares is the disjoint union  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA rectangular tile cannot contain an uncovered square; therefore every tile lies wholly in \\(L\\) or wholly in \\(R\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}= \\#\\text{tiles covering }L \\;+\\; \\#\\text{tiles covering }R .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the left side  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=p(i)-1\\qquad(0\\le\\ell_i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\{\\ell_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}= \\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus exactly \\(n-1\\) rows have \\(\\ell_i>0\\), and those positive lengths are pairwise distinct.\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(i,\\ell_i)\\mid \\ell_i>0\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the rightmost cell of each non\u2011empty left block.  \n\n**Claim.** No rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can contain two distinct cells of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(T\\subseteq L\\) contains \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) and \\((k,\\ell_k)\\) with \\(\\ell_i\\neq\\ell_k\\).  \nAssume \\(\\ell_i>\\ell_k\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) is axis\u2011aligned, its column interval contains both columns \\(\\ell_k\\) and \\(\\ell_i\\); therefore it also contains column \\(\\ell_i\\) in row \\(k\\), i.e. the cell \\((k,\\ell_i)\\).  \nBut \\(\\ell_i>\\ell_k\\) and the length of row \\(k\\)\u2019s left block is \\(\\ell_k\\); hence \\((k,\\ell_i)\\notin L\\), contradicting \\(T\\subseteq L\\). \u220e\n\nBecause the cells of \\(S\\) are pairwise incomparable, each rectangle of \\(L\\) can cover at most one of them.  \nSince \\(|S|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(L\\).\n\nThe bound is tight: covering each left block by a single horizontal rectangle (one per row with \\(\\ell_i>0\\)) uses exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  The right side  \n\nDefine for each row  \n\n\\[\nr_i:=n-p(i)\\qquad(0\\le r_i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(\\{r_i\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); thus exactly \\(n-1\\) rows have \\(r_i>0\\) and the rightmost cells of those blocks form an antichain of size \\(n-1\\).  \nRepeating the argument of the previous section shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(R\\), and this bound is attained by horizontal rectangles covering each right block.\n\nConsequently, by (2),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n- For each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  H_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n  \\]\n\n  which covers the left block of row \\(i\\).\n\n- For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  K_i=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  which covers the right block of row \\(i\\).\n\nThe rectangles \\(H_i\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(L\\); the rectangles \\(K_i\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(R\\).  \nNo rectangle contains a diagonal cell, and every non\u2011diagonal cell belongs to exactly one rectangle.  \nThus this tiling uses  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound (4) and the explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
