tangles needed. This argument holds if we consider any rectangle that covers any squares in L (including those that also cover squares in R). Does the claim that each rectangle can contain at most one cell of S hold for any rectangle that covers squares in L (even if it also covers squares in R)? Let's examine: Suppose we have a rectangle that includes squares from both L and R, and includes two cells from S. Could that happen? S consists of cells (i, ℓ_i) where ℓ_i = p(i)-1, i.e., the rightmost cell of the left block of row i. These cells are in L. The rectangle's column interval must contain ℓ_i for each such cell. If the rectangle includes two such cells (i, ℓ_i) and (k, ℓ_k) with ℓ_i ≠ ℓ_k, then the rectangle's column interval includes both ℓ_i and ℓ_k. Since ℓ_i and ℓ_k are distinct, the column interval includes all columns between them, including possibly p(i) or p(k). But ℓ_i = p(i)-1, ℓ_k = p(k)-1. So ℓ_i < p(i) and ℓ_k < p(k). The rectangle's column interval includes ℓ_i and ℓ_k. Does it also include p(i) or p(k)? If ℓ_i < ℓ_k, then the interval includes columns from ℓ_i to ℓ_k. Since ℓ_i = p(i)-1, the interval includes column p(i)-1 and maybe p(i) if p(i) ≤ ℓ_k? But ℓ_k = p(k)-1, which could be larger than p(i). If ℓ_k ≥ p(i), then the interval includes p(i). But p(i) is the uncovered column for row i. If row i is included in the rectangle's row interval, then the rectangle would contain the uncovered square (i,p(i)), which is not allowed. However, the rectangle might not include row i? But the rectangle includes cell (i,ℓ_i), so it includes row i. So row i is in the rectangle. So if ℓ_k ≥ p(i), then rectangle includes column p(i) and row i, thus includes uncovered square (i,p(i)), which is not allowed. So to avoid that, we must have ℓ_k < p(i). Since ℓ_k = p(k)-1, this condition is p(k)-1 < p(i). Similarly, we need ℓ_i < p(k) to avoid uncovered square (k,p(k)). Since ℓ_i = p(i)-1, condition is p(i)-1 < p(k). So we need p(k)-1 < p(i) and p(i)-1 < p(k). The