and a right square in row k at column j' > p(k), then the rectangle's column interval includes all columns between j and j', inclusive. Since j < p(i) and j' > p(k), the interval includes columns that might be in between. But does it guarantee that the rectangle includes an uncovered square? Possibly not if the uncovered squares are at different columns. Let's examine: Suppose p(i) = 5, p(k) = 7. A left square in row i could be at column 3 (j=3). A right square in row k could be at column 9 (j'=9). The rectangle would have column interval [3,9] and row interval [i,k] (assuming i < k). Does this rectangle contain any uncovered squares? The uncovered squares are at (i,5) and (k,7). Both are within the rectangle's column interval [3,9] and row interval [i,k], so the rectangle would contain both uncovered squares. So indeed any rectangle that contains a left square from one row and a right square from another row would also contain the uncovered squares of those rows, because the rectangle's column interval covers the columns of the uncovered squares (since they lie between left and right). However, we need to be careful: If the left square is from row i and the right square is from row k, it's possible that p(i) > p(k) or p(i) < p(k). But the rectangle's column interval will cover all columns between the leftmost column and rightmost column of the rectangle. If the left square's column is less than p(i) and the right square's column is greater than p(k), then the interval includes p(i) and p(k) (since p(i) is between left square's column and p(i) maybe not? Actually, if left square column j < p(i), then p(i) > j. If right square column j' > p(k), then p(k) < j'. So the interval [j, j'] includes both p(i) and p(k) if j <= p(i) <= j' and j <= p(k) <= j'. Since j < p(i) and p(k) < j', we have j <= p(i) and p(k) <= j'. However, we need to ensure that p(i) <= j' and p(k) >= j. Since j' > p(k) and j < p(i), we have j' > p(k) and j < p(i). But it's possible that p(i) > j' if 