quivalent to partitioning the set of covered squares into axis-aligned rectangles.\n\nWe need to find the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix.\n\nThe solution claims that the minimal number is 2n-2 = 4048. Let's verify if that is plausible.\n\nWe can think about known results: Partitioning a chessboard with a missing rook (like a permutation matrix) into rectangles. There's a known result: The complement of a permutation matrix (i.e., the cells not in the permutation) can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles in each of the two regions left and right of the permutation, giving total 2n-2 rectangles. This seems plausible. But we need to verify that no better partition exists (i.e., fewer rectangles). The solution gives a lower bound argument: each column of L forces a distinct rectangle, giving at least n-1 rectangles for L; similarly for R. That seems plausible. Let's examine the lower bound argument.\n\nDefine L = cells left of the permutation (j < pi(i)), R = cells right of the permutation (j > pi(i)). So L and R are two disjoint monotone regions: L is a Young diagram shape (maybe not monotone but each row i has left part up to pi(i)-1). Similarly R is the right part.\n\nNow they argue that each column c (1 ≤ c ≤ n-1) has a topmost cell in L, at row i_c = min {i | pi(i) > c}. That is the first row where the uncovered column pi(i) is to the right of column c, so column c is in L for rows i >= i_c? Actually, for rows i < i_c, pi(i) ≤ c, so column c is not left of pi(i) (since j < pi(i) condition fails). For row i_c, pi(i_c) > c, so column c is left of pi(i_c) and thus belongs to L. So (i_c, c) is the topmost cell in column c belonging to L. Good.\n\nNow they consider the rectangle covering that cell. They argue that the rectangle cannot extend above row i_c because above that row column c is not in L; similarly cannot extend to column c+1 because column c+1 is not in L for row i_c (since pi(i_c) ≤ c+1). Actually they claim pi(i_c) ≤ c+1, so column c+1 is not in L for row i_c. Let's verify: pi(i_c) > c (by definition). Since pi(i_c) is a permutation, it's an integer between 1 and n. So pi(i_c) could be c+1, c+2, ..., n. If pi(i_c) = c+1, then column c+1 is not in L for row i_c (since j < pi(i_c) means j ≤ c). If pi(i_c) > c+1, then column c+1 is also < pi(i_c) (since c+1 < pi(i_c)), so column c+1 would be in L for that row. So the claim that column c+1 is not in L for row i_c is not always true; it's only true if pi(i_c) = c+1. However, they claim pi(i_c) ≤ c+1, which would be false in general; we only know pi(i_c) > c, not that it's ≤ c+1. So the argument that the rectangle cannot extend to column c+1 might be flawed. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nThe rectangle containing (i_c, c) must be wholly inside L. The rectangle can extend to the right as far as the rightmost column of L in row i_c, which is pi(i_c)-1. So the rightmost column of the rectangle can be at most pi(i_c)-1. Since c ≤ pi(i_c)-1, the rectangle could potentially extend to column pi(i_c)-1, which could be > c. So the rectangle could include columns beyond c, as long as they are still left of pi(i_c). So the claim that the rectangle's rightmost column must be exactly c is not necessarily true. The argument uses a property that pi(i_c) ≤ c+1, which is not guaranteed.\n\nThus the lower bound argument may be flawed. Let's see if the lower bound can be salvaged: perhaps they intended to define i_c differently, maybe i_c = min {i | pi(i) > c} ensures that for row i_c, pi(i_c) is the smallest uncovered column that is greater than c. Since pi(i_c) > c, but there could be other rows i > i_c where pi(i) is also > c. But the minimal i ensures that for rows above i_c, pi(i) ≤ c; for row i_c, pi(i_c) > c. However, pi(i_c) could be any value > c, not necessarily c+1. So the rectangle containing (i_c, c) could extend to column pi(i_c)-1, which could be > c.\n\nThus the claim that each column c forces a distinct rectangle with 