                    "reasoning_content": "We need to verify the provided solution for the problem: \"Consider a 2025×2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of different sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nThe solution claims the answer is 4048. It uses a combinatorial argument: define uncovered squares as a permutation matrix; define L and R as left and right of the permutation; each tile must be wholly in L or R; then lower bound number of rectangles needed to partition L and R each at least n-1, giving total at least 2n-2 = 4048. Then constructs a tiling achieving exactly 2n-2 rectangles.\n\nWe need to apply the hierarchical decision tree.\n\nFirst, Level 1: Check answer correctness. The solution says answer is 4048. Is that correct? We need to verify if the answer is indeed correct. We need to evaluate if the solution's answer matches the correct answer.\n\nWe need to determine if the answer 4048 is indeed the minimum number of tiles. Let's think about the problem.\n\nWe have a 2025x2025 grid. We need to place rectangular tiles (axis-aligned, sides on grid lines) covering some squares, with each unit square covered at most once (i.e., tiles are non-overlapping). Each row must have exactly one uncovered unit square, and each column must have exactly one uncovered unit square. So uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: exactly one uncovered per row and per column. So there are 2025 uncovered squares, each in a distinct row and column.\n\nWe need to tile the remaining squares (the covered squares) with axis-aligned rectangles. The question: what is the minimum number of rectangles needed to cover all squares except those 2025 uncovered squares, with no overlap? Equivalent to partitioning the set of covered squares into axis-aligned rectangles.\n\nWe need to find the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix.\n\nThe solution claims that the minimal number is 2n-2 = 4048. Let's verify if that is plausible.\n\nWe can think about known results: Partitioning a chessboard with a missing rook (like a permutation matrix) into rectangles. There's a known result: The complement of a permutation matrix (i.e., the cells not in the permutation) can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles in each of the two regions left and right of the permutation, giving total 2n-2 rectangles. This seems plausible. But we need to verify that no better partition exists (i.e., fewer rectangles). The solution gives a lower bound argument: each column of L forces a distinct rectangle, giving at least n-1 rectangles for L; similarly for R. That seems plausible. Let's examine the lower bound argument.\n\nDefine L = cells left of the permutation (j < pi(i)), R = cells right of the permutation (j > pi(i)). So L and R are two disjoint monotone regions: L is a Young diagram shape (maybe not monotone but each row i has left part up to pi(i)-1). Similarly R is the right part.\n\nNow they argue that each column c (1 ≤ c ≤ n-1) has a topmost cell in L, at row i_c = min {i | pi(i) > c}. That is the first row where the uncovered column pi(i) is to the right of column c, so column c is in L for rows i >= i_c? Actually, for rows i < i_c, pi(i) ≤ c, so column c is not left of pi(i) (since j < pi(i) condition fails). For row i_c, pi(i_c) > c, so column c is left of pi(i_c) and thus belongs to L. So (i_c, c) is the topmost cell in column c belonging to L. Good.\n\nNow they consider the rectangle covering that cell. They argue that the rectangle cannot extend above row i_c because above that row column c is not in L; similarly cannot extend to column c+1 because column c+1 is not in L for row i_c (since pi(i_c) ≤ c+1). Actually t