n attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\).  \n   Then \\(\\ell_i=i-1\\) and \\(r_i=n-i\\).  \n   - For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     L_k=\\{(i,k):i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     i.e. the rectangle \\([k+1,n]\\times\\{k\\}\\) of size \\((n-k)\\times1\\).  \n   - For each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     R_k=\\{(i,k):i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\},\n     \\]\n     i.e. the rectangle \\([1,k-1]\\times\\{k\\}\\) of size \\((k-1)\\times1\\).  \n\n   The \\(L_k\\)’s cover all squares left of the main diagonal, the \\(R_k\\)’s cover all squares right of the diagonal, and the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered.  \n   The total number of tiles is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is attainable, so it is optimal.  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the condition must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=p(i)-1\\qquad\\text{(number of covered squares to the left of the hole)},\n\\]\n\\[\nr_i:=n-p(i)\\qquad\\text{(number of covered squares to the right of the hole)} .\n\\]\n\nThe covered squares split into two disjoint regions:\n\n\\[\nL:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le \\ell_i\\},\n\\qquad\nR:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\{(i,j):p(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) consists of the squares left of the holes, and \\(R\\) consists of the squares right of the holes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Left‑tiles).\n\nEvery tile that lies entirely inside \\(L\\) has the form  \n\n\\[\nT=[a,b]\\time