y inside \\(L\\) has the form  \n\n\\[\nT=[a,b]\\times[1,d]\n\\]\n\nwith integers \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le d\\le n-1\\).  \nConsequently, for each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there must be at least one left\u2011tile whose rightmost column is exactly \\(k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(T\\) be a tile contained in \\(L\\). Its column interval must be a subset of \\([1,n]\\) that contains no hole of any row it covers. Since all holes of the rows covered by \\(T\\) lie strictly to the right of the tile, the tile\u2019s column interval must be of the form \\([1,d]\\) for some \\(d\\).  \n\nFix \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\). Because the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), there exists a row \\(i_k\\) with \\(\\ell_{i_k}=k\\); i.e. row \\(i_k\\) has exactly the squares \\((i_k,1),\\dots ,(i_k,k)\\) in \\(L\\) and no square \\((i_k,k+1)\\). The square \\((i_k,k)\\) must be covered by a left\u2011tile. Any left\u2011tile covering \\((i_k,k)\\) has rightmost column at least \\(k\\); if it were larger than \\(k\\) the tile would also have to contain \\((i_k,k+1)\\), which does not belong to \\(L\\). Hence the rightmost column of that tile is exactly \\(k\\). \u220e\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of left\u2011tiles}\\;\\ge\\;|\\{k:1\\le k\\le n-1\\}|=n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Right\u2011tiles).\n\nSymmetrically, every tile contained in \\(R\\) has the form  \n\n\\[\nT=[a,b]\\times[c,n]\n\\]\n\nwith \\(1\\le c\\le n\\). Moreover, for each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there must be at least one right\u2011tile whose leftmost column is \\(n-k+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply Lemma\u202f1 to the board reflected horizontally (replace column \\(j\\) by column \\(n+1-j\\)). The reflected left\u2011region becomes \\(R\\); the statement follows after undoing the reflection. \u220e\n\nConsequently,\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of right\u2011tiles}\\;\\ge\\;n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for the total number of tiles.\n\nAdding (1) and (2) yields\n\n\\[\n\\text{total number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound.\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen \\(\\ell_i=i-1\\) and \\(r_i=n-i\\).  \n\n*Left\u2011region.*  \nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nL_k:=\\{(i,k):i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\n      =[k+1,n]\\times\\{k\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\((n-k)\\times1\\).  \nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares with column index \\(<\\) row index, i.e. the whole left\u2011region \\(L\\).\n\n*Right\u2011region.*  \nFor each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_k:=\\{(i,k):i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\n      =[1,k-1]\\times\\{k\\},\n\\]\n\nof size \\((k-1)\\times1\\).  \nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint, cover the squares with column index \\(>\\) row index, i.e. the right\u2011region \\(R\\).\n\nThe diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered; each row and each column contains exactly one such square.  \nThe total number of tiles used is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Optimality.\n\nThe construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, matching the lower bound (3).  \nTherefore \\(2n-2\\) is the minimal possible number of tiles.\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
