"### Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The minimum number of tiles that Matilda must place is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Reformulation.**  \n   The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix: there is a bijection \\(\\pi\\colon\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  All other squares must be partitioned into axis\u2011parallel rectangles (tiles) that avoid every hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound.**  \n   Define the two sets of \u201cadjacent\u201d squares  \n\n   \\[\n   L=\\{(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\mid \\pi(i)>1\\},\\qquad \n   R=\\{(i,\\pi(i)+1)\\mid \\pi(i)<n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Both sets contain exactly \\(n-1\\) squares, and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).  \n   *Claim.* No rectangle can contain two distinct squares of \\(L\\cup R\\).  \n   \u2013 If a rectangle contained two squares of \\(L\\), say \\((i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\((j,\\pi(j)-1)\\) with \\(i<j\\), then its column interval would contain \\(\\pi(i)\\), forcing the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) to lie inside \u2013 impossible.  \n   \u2013 The same argument works for two squares of \\(R\\).  \n   \u2013 A rectangle cannot contain one square from \\(L\\) and one from \\(R\\) because its column interval would have to be simultaneously left of \\(\\pi(i)\\) and right of \\(\\pi(j)\\), which forces inclusion of a hole.  \n\n   Hence each tile can contain **at most one** element of \\(L\\cup R\\).  Since every element of \\(L\\cup R\\) must be covered, the total number of tiles is at least  \n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cup R|=|L|+|R|=2(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the cyclic permutation  \n\n   \\[\n   \\pi(i)=\\begin{cases}\n   i+1 & (1\\le i\\le n-1),\\\\\n   1   & (i=n).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   *Horizontal tiles.* For each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)<n\\) (i.e. \\(i\\neq n-1\\)) place a horizontal tile covering all squares to the **right** of the hole:\n\n   \\[\n   H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   These are \\(n-1\\) disjoint horizontal tiles.\n\n   *Vertical tiles.* For each column \\(j\\) with \\(j\\neq n\\) place a vertical tile covering all squares to the **left** of the hole in that column:\n\n   \\[\n   V_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   These are also \\(n-1\\) disjoint vertical tiles.  \n\n   The horizontal family lies strictly to the right of every hole, the vertical family lies strictly to the left of every hole, and the two families are disjoint.  Every non\u2011hole square belongs either to some \\(H_i\\) (right side) or to some \\(V_j\\) (left side).  Thus all \\(n^{2}-n\\) covered squares are partitioned into exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound shows that any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the explicit construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  For the given grid with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares, i.e. the set  \n\n\\[\nC=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U,\n\\]\n\nmust be partitioned into axis\u2011parallel rectangles (tiles) whose interiors are disjoint and which contain no element of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A universal lower bound\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\mid \\pi(i)>1\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,\\pi(i)+1)\\mid \\pi(i)<n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) and exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=n\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n|L|=|R|=n-1,\\qquad L\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.** *No rectangle can contain two distinct squares of \\(L\\cup R\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(T\\) contains two distinct squares of \\(L\\), say  \n\n\\[\na=(i,\\pi(i)-1),\\qquad b=(j,\\pi(j)-1),\\qquad i<j .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, \\(\\pi(i)\\neq\\pi(j)\\).  Without loss of generality \\(\\pi(i)<\\pi(j)\\).  \nThe column interval of \\(T\\) contains both \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) and \\(\\pi(j)-1\\); hence it also contains the column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  Because row \\(i\\) belongs to \\(T\\), the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\in U\\) lies inside \\(T\\), contradicting the requirement that a tile may not contain a hole.  Thus two squares of \\(L\\) cannot belong to the same rectangle.\n\nThe same argument with the roles of \u201c\\(-1\\)\u201d replaced by \u201c\\(+1\\)\u201d shows that two squares of \\(R\\) cannot belong to the same rectangle.\n\nFinally, suppose \\(T\\) contains a square \\(a\\in L\\) from row \\(i\\) and a square \\(b\\in R\\) from row \\(j\\).  \nThe column of \\(a\\) is \\(\\le\\pi(i)-1\\) while the column of \\(b\\) is \\(\\ge\\pi(j)+1\\).  \nHence the column interval of \\(T\\) contains the whole interval \\([\\pi(i),\\pi(j)]\\).  \nIf \\(\\pi(i)\\le\\pi(j)\\) then \\((i,\\pi(i))\\in U\\) lies in \\(T\\); if \\(\\pi(j)<\\pi(i)\\) then \\((j,\\pi(j))\\in U\\) lies in \\(T\\).  In either case a hole is contained, a contradiction.  \u220e\n\n---\n\nBecause every element of \\(L\\cup R\\) is a non\u2011hole square, each must belong to some tile.  By Lemma\u202f1 each tile can contain **at most one** element of \\(L\\cup R\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;|L\\cup R|=|L|+|R|=2(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nTake the *cyclic* permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(i)=\\begin{cases}\ni+1 & (1\\le i\\le n-1),\\\\[2mm]\n1   & (i=n).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe holes are \\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n-1,n),(n,1)\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)<n\\) (i.e. \\(i\\neq n-1\\)) define  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) is a (possibly empty) horizontal rectangle lying strictly to the right of the hole in row \\(i\\).  \nFor \\(i=n-1\\) the set is empty, so no tile is needed.  \nThus we obtain exactly \\(n-1\\) non\u2011empty horizontal tiles, each covering all squares to the right of the corresponding hole.\n\n*Vertical tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(j\\neq n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_j\\) is a vertical rectangle lying strictly to the left of the hole in column \\(j\\); it contains all squares of column \\(j\\) that are left of the unique hole \\((j+1,j)\\).  \nThese are \\(n-1\\) disjoint vertical tiles.\n\n*Verification.*  \n- The families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are disjoint because every \\(H_i\\) uses only columns \\(> \\pi(i)\\) while every \\(V_j\\) uses only columns \\(\\le j\\), and for each row \\(i\\) we have \\(j\\le\\pi(i)-1\\) for all columns in \\(V_j\\).  \n- Every square of \\(C\\) is either to the right of its row\u2019s hole (hence belongs to the appropriate \\(H_i\\)) or to the left of its row\u2019s hole (hence belongs to the appropriate \\(V_j\\)).  \n- No tile contains a hole, because each \\(H_i\\) stays strictly to the right of \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) and each \\(V_j\\) stays strictly to the left of \\((\\pi^{-1}(j),j)\\).\n\nTherefore the \\(2n-2\\) tiles \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i\\neq n-1}\\cup\\{V_j\\}_{j\\neq n}\\) form a valid tiling of the grid.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nInequality (1) shows that any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Hence the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Application to the given grid\n\nFor the problem we have \\(n=2025\\).  Substituting into the formula yields  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
