ost column is at most min_{i∈I} (σ(i)-1). So to cover column k, we need a set of rows I such that min_{i∈I} (σ(i)-1) ≥ k, i.e., all those rows have holes to the right of column k. Then we can choose J = [1, k] (or [c,k]) as column interval. So we could cover many columns with one rectangle if we choose rows with holes far to the right.\n\nThus the lower bound argument that we need n-1 left-justified rectangles may be too strong. We need to refine.\n\nLet's attempt to prove a lower bound of 2n-2 using a combinatorial argument like counting \"breakpoints\" or \"adjacent pairs\". Possibly we can use the concept of \"alternating runs\" of permutation.\n\nDefine permutation σ. Consider the set of \"inversions\" or \"adjacent pairs\" where σ(i) < σ(i+1). Actually any permutation can be decomposed into increasing runs. For each increasing run, we can cover the left part of those rows with a single left-justified rectangle? Let's think.\n\nIf we have rows i1 < i2 < ... < ik such that σ(i1) < σ(i2) < ... < σ(ik) (i.e., increasing in both row and column indices), then the left intervals L_i = [1, σ(i)-1] are nested: L_i1 ⊆ L_i2 ⊆ ... ⊆ L_ik (since σ(i) increasing). So the intersection of left intervals for any subset of these rows is the smallest one, i.e., L_i1. So we could cover all left parts of these rows with a rectangle covering rows i1..ik and columns 1..σ(i1)-1. That rectangle would be left-justified for all those rows. So we can combine rows that form a decreasing (or increasing?) sequence. Similarly, for decreasing runs we can combine right parts.\n\nThus the number of left-justified rectangles needed is at least the number of \"maximal decreasing sequences\" of σ? Actually we need to partition rows into groups such that within each group, the left intervals intersect nontrivially (i.e., there is a column interval that is subset of all L_i). The maximal groups correspond to decreasing sequences of σ? Let's examine.\n\nGiven rows i < j. L_i = [1, σ(i)-1], L_j = [1, σ(j)-1]. Intersection is [1, min(σ(i),σ(j))-1]. So it's nonempty if min(σ(i),σ(j)) > 1. That's always nonempty unless one of σ(i) = 1. But the intersection is larger for smaller σ. So to have a large common left interval, we need the smallest σ among the rows to be large enough. So grouping rows with small σ values restricts the left interval.\n\nBut we need to cover all left cells. Perhaps we can think of a bipartite graph representation: rows on left, columns on right, edges for cells to be covered. The complement of a permutation matrix is a complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching. We want to partition the edges into complete bipartite subgraphs (i.e., rectangles). The minimum number of complete bipartite subgraphs needed to partition the edges of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is known as the \"biclique partition number\" of the complement of a perfect matching. This is a known combinatorial problem. The biclique partition number of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is exactly 2n - 2. Indeed, there is a known result: The edge set of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching can be partitioned into 2n-2 complete bipartite subgraphs, and this is optimal. This is a classic result in extremal graph theory. The construction is exactly the one we gave: For each i, a star from vertex i on left to all right vertices except its matched partner, and similarly for each right vertex. But those stars are not rectangles? Actually a star is a complete bipartite subgraph K_{1,m} which corresponds to a rectangle of size 1×m (horizontal tile) or m×1 (vertical tile). So the construction uses n-1 horizontal stars (rows) and n-1 vertical stars (columns). That's 2n-2. And it's known that you cannot do with fewer because each vertex must appear in at least two bicliques? Let's recall known results: The biclique partition number of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is 2n-2. This is a known theorem: The edge covering number (biclique partition) of the complement of a perfect matching in a complete bipartite graph is 2n-2. The proof uses a counting argument: each biclique can cover at most one missing edge (i.e., the perfect matching edge) per row/column? Actually each biclique can miss at most one vertex on each side? Let's derive.\n\nConsider the bipartite graph G = K_{n,n} \\ M where M is a perfect matching (the holes). We want to partition its edges into bicliques (complete bipartite subgraphs). Equivalent to partition the complement matrix into rectangles. The biclique partition number is denoted bp(G). Known result: bp(K_{n,n} \\ M) = 2n - 2. The proof: Lower bound: Each biclique can contain at most n-1 edges incident to any vertex? Wait, each vertex in G has degree n-1 (since it's missing exactly one edge, its matched partner). In a biclique partition, each vertex's incident edges are partitioned among the bicliques that contain that vertex. In each biclique, a vertex can be incident to at most all vertices on the opposite side that are present in that biclique. But we need to count the number of bicliques needed to cover all edges incident to a given vertex. Since each vertex is missing exactly one opposite vertex, we need to ensure that the missing edge is not covered. For each vertex on left side, its incident edges to all right vertices except its matched partner must be covered. In any biclique, if the left vertex appears, then the biclique includes a set of right vertices. To avoid covering the missing edge, the biclique cannot include the matched partner on the right side if it includes that left vertex. So each left vertex can appear in bicliques that avoid its matched partner. Similarly for right vertices.\n\nNow, we can think of each biclique as either \"left-star\" (one left vertex, many right vertices) or \"right-star\" (many left vertices, one right vertex) or more general. But perhaps any biclique that includes more than one left vertex must also include more than one right vertex. But then for each left vertex, the biclique must avoid its matched partner. So the set of right vertices in the biclique cannot include the matched partner of any left vertex present. So the right side of the biclique must be a subset of the complement of the matched partners of the left vertices. Similarly, the left side must be a subset of complement of matched partners of right vertices. This imposes constraints.\n\nOne known lower bound argument: In any biclique partition of K_{n,n} \\ M, each biclique can be assigned a \"type\": either left-star (i.e., left side size = 1) or right-star (right side size = 1) or both sides >1. Show that any biclique with both sides >1 can be replaced by two bicliques of star type without increasing the total number. Then we can assume an optimal partition uses only stars. Then we need at least n-1 left stars and n-1 right stars. This yields lower bound 2n-2.\n\nLet's attempt to prove that any biclique partition of K_{n,n} \\ M can be transformed into one where each biclique is a star (i.e., one side size 1). Then we can count stars needed.\n\nAlternatively, we can directly prove lower bound: In any partition, each row must be involved in at least one horizontal tile (i.e., rectangle with height 1) and each column must be involved in at least one vertical tile (rectangle with width 1). But maybe a rectangle can be both horizontal and vertical (i.e., a single cell). But that would be both a row and column tile. However, we need to cover all cells, not just one per row/column. Actually each row has many cells to cover (n-1 cells). We need to cover them with rectangles. If a rectangle includes multiple rows, it must be vertical (width 1) or else it would cross holes. Wait, a rectangle that includes multiple rows and multiple columns must avoid holes in each of those rows. As argued earlier, if a rectangle includes multiple rows and multiple columns, then for each row, its column interval must be either left of hole or right of hole. If the rectangle includes multiple rows, the column interval must be a subset of the intersection of left intervals of some rows and right intervals of others. If there are rows with holes on both sides of the column interval, the interval must be a single column (or single row). So a rectangle with both dimensions >1 can only exist if all rows covered have holes on the same side of the column interval (i.e., all holes to the right or all holes to the left). In that case, the rectangle is left-justified or right-justified. So such rectangles are possible.\n\nThus we can have rectangles that are \"wide\" (multiple columns) but only cover rows whose holes are all to the right (or all to the left). Similarly, rectangles that are \"tall\" (multiple rows) but only cover columns whose holes are all above (or below). So we can have both types.\n\nNow, to get a lower bound, we can consider the graph of rows vs columns, and consider the \"cut\" at each column k. For each column k, consider the set of rows with holes to the right of column k (i.e., σ(i) > k). Those rows need to have column k covered. The only way to cover column k for those rows is by rectangles that are left-justified (i.e., column interval subset of L_i). Since column k is the rightmost column of those left intervals for some rows (maybe not). Actually for each row i with σ(i) > k, column k is within L_i. So we need to cover the cell (i,k). These cells across those rows form a vertical segment (maybe not contiguous rows). But we can group them into rectangles. A left-justified rectangle covering column k can have column interval J that ends at some column ≥ k (but must be ≤ min_{i∈I} (σ(i)-1)). If we want to cover column k for a set of rows I, we need J to include column k. So we can choose J = [c,k] where c ≤ k. The rectangle's rightmost column is k. So each left-justified rectangle that covers column k must have its rightmost column exactly k (or larger but then it would also cover column >k, which might be okay). But if it covers column >k, then for rows with σ(i) > k, column >k is also in L_i (since σ(i)-1 ≥ k+1). So a rectangle could have rightmost column >k and still cover column k. However, we need to ensure that for rows with holes to the left of column k (σ(i) < k), the rectangle does not include them because then column interval would intersect the hole? Actually if rectangle includes rows with holes left of column k (σ(i) < k), then column interval must be subset of R_i (right side) for those rows, which would require leftmost column ≥ σ(i)+1 ≤ k. So column interval could include column k as well. So a rectangle could include rows from both sides as long as its column interval is exactly {k} (single column). So again, we need to consider vertical strips.\n\nThus for each column k, we need to cover all cells (i,k) where i runs over all rows except possibly the row where σ(i)=k (hole). So we need to cover n-1 cells in column k. These cells can be covered by a combination of vertical strips (width 1) and horizontal rectangles (height 1) that intersect column k. Horizontal rectangles (height 1) cover a single row across multiple columns, and can include column k for rows where σ(i) ≠ k. So each horizontal rectangle covering row i and column k also covers other columns in that row. So we can use horizontal rectangles to cover many cells in column k across different rows? Actually a horizontal rectangle covers a single row, so it covers only one cell in column k (the cell at that row). So to cover all n-1 cells in column k, we need at least n-1 rectangles (each covering one cell) unless we use vertical rectangles that cover multiple rows in column k. A vertical rectangle of width 1 can cover many rows in column k. So we could cover column k with a single vertical rectangle covering all rows except the hole row. However, such a vertical rectangle would have column interval {k} and rows I = all rows except the hole row. But does this rectangle intersect any hole? The hole row is excluded, so it's fine. But does the rectangle intersect any other hole? The holes are at positions (i,σ(i)). For any row i in I, σ(i) ≠ k (by definition). So column k is not a hole for those rows. So the vertical rectangle covering column k and all rows except the hole row is allowed. So we could cover each column with a single vertical rectangle (except maybe the column where the hole is at row i). But note that vertical rectangle covering column k for all rows except the hole row would intersect holes in other rows? No, because holes are at distinct columns per row, so column k is not a hole for any other row. So a vertical rectangle of width 1 covering column k and all rows except the hole row is a valid tile. However, this rectangle would intersect the row i where σ(i) = k? Actually that row is excluded, so it's fine.\n\nThus we could cover each column with a single vertical rectangle, leading to n rectangles total. But we also need to cover the rows' right parts? Wait, covering each column with a vertical rectangle would cover all cells in that column except the hole. That would cover all cells of the grid except the holes. So we would have n rectangles (vertical strips). But is this allowed? Let's test with n=2: vertical strips for column 1 covering row 2 only (since row 1 hole at column 1). For column 2 covering row 1 only (since row 2 hole at column 2). That's two vertical rectangles, each covering a single cell. That's indeed a valid tiling with 2 rectangles, which matches the lower bound 2n-2 = 2. So n=2 works.\n\nBut for n=3, can we cover each column with a vertical rectangle? Let's try with identity permutation (holes on diagonal). Column 1: hole at (1,1). So vertical rectangle covering column 1 rows 2-3 (rows 2 and 3). This rectangle includes cells (2,1) and (3,1). Both are not holes. That's fine. Column 2: hole at (2,2). Vertical rectangle covering rows 1 and 3: cells (1,2) and (3,2). That's fine. Column 3: hole at (3,3). Vertical rectangle covering rows 1 and 2: cells (1,3) and (2,3). That's fine. So we have 3 vertical rectangles covering all non-diagonal cells. That's only 3 rectangles, which is less than 4 (2n-2). Wait, but earlier we argued that for n=3, we need at least 4 rectangles. Did we make a mistake? Let's check: The vertical rectangles as described are width 1, height 2 (except maybe height 2). They are disjoint? Let's examine: Column 1 rectangle covers rows 2-3. Column 2 rectangle covers rows 1 and 3. Column 3 rectangle covers rows 1 and 2. Are these rectangles overlapping? They are in different columns, so they are disjoint (no overlapping squares). So they form a partition of the complement. Indeed, each non-diagonal cell is covered exactly once. So we have a tiling with 3 rectangles! That contradicts earlier claim that min is 4. Let's verify if any rule prohibits a rectangle covering non-consecutive rows? The problem says each tile is a rectangle with sides on grid lines. A rectangle covering rows 2-3 in column 1 is contiguous rows (2 and 3) and contiguous columns (just column 1). That's a rectangle of size 2×1. That's fine. Similarly, column 2 rectangle covering rows 1 and 3 is not contiguous rows (rows 1 and 3 are not consecutive because row 2 is missing). Wait, a rectangle must be contiguous in both dimensions: the set of rows must be a consecutive interval. The rectangle covering rows 1 and 3 but not row 2 is not a rectangle because there would be a gap. The rectangle would be rows 1-3 inclusive, which includes row 2, but row 2 column 2 is a hole (since (2,2) is a hole). So we cannot include row 2 because that would include the hole. So we cannot have a rectangle covering rows 1 and 3 only; we would need to include row 2, which would include a hole. So the rectangle covering column 2 cannot be a single rectangle covering rows 1 and 3 only. So we need to split column 2 into two separate rectangles: one covering row 1 (cell (1,2)) and another covering row 3 (cell (3,2)). Since they are not contiguous rows, we need separate rectangles. So the vertical strip approach fails because we cannot skip the hole row. The rectangle must be contiguous rows. So for each column, we need to partition the rows into intervals that avoid the hole row. Since the hole row is a single row, the column can be covered by at most two rectangles: one covering rows above the hole, one covering rows below the hole. So each column can be covered by at most two rectangles. For identity permutation, column k has hole at row k, so we need two rectangles: one covering rows 1..k-1 (above) and one covering rows k+1..n (below). That's exactly the construction we gave: vertical tiles for each column covering rows below the diagonal (k+1..n) and horizontal tiles for each row covering columns to the right (i+1..n). Wait, vertical tiles for each column covering rows below diagonal are n-1 rectangles (for columns 1..n-1). The rows above diagonal are covered by horizontal tiles. So each column is covered by two rectangles: one vertical (below) and one horizontal (above). But we could also cover the above part with vertical rectangles for columns above? Let's think.\n\nThus the minimal number of rectangles is at least n-1 + n-1 = 2n-2, because each column must be covered by at least one rectangle for the part below the hole and one for the part above the hole, and each such part cannot be merged across columns because the holes are at different rows for each column. However, maybe we could merge the above parts across columns into horizontal rectangles (as we did). So the lower bound is 2n-2.\n\nBut we need to prove that we cannot do better than 2n-2 for any permutation. Let's attempt a rigorous proof.\n\nObservation: For each column j, the cells in column j that need to be covered are all rows i ≠ σ^{-1}(j) (i.e., all rows except the one whose hole is in column j). The set of rows to be covered in column j is split into two intervals: rows above the hole (i < σ^{-1}(j)) and rows below the hole (i > σ^{-1}(j)). Since any rectangle covering cells in column j must consist of a contiguous set of rows (an interval). Therefore, the cells above the hole must be covered by rectangles whose column interval includes column j and whose row interval lies entirely above the hole. Similarly for cells below the hole. Thus each column j requires at least two rectangles unless the hole is at the topmost or bottommost row (i.e., σ^{-1}(j) = 1 or n). In that case, the column has only one interval (all rows either below or above). So each column contributes at least 1 rectangle, and columns where the hole is not at an extreme contribute at least 2 rectangles. Summing over all columns, we get a lower bound.\n\nBut we can also consider rows similarly. However, we need to avoid double counting. Let's attempt to count the total number of \"segments\" (maximal contiguous blocks of cells in a column that need to be covered). For each column j, there are either 1 or 2 such segments, depending on whether the hole is at the top or bottom. Similarly for each row i, there are either 1 or 2 segments horizontally. The total number of segments across all rows and columns is something like 2n - c where c is number of holes at extremes? Actually each hole at (i,σ(i)) splits its row into two horizontal segments (left and right) and its column into two vertical segments (above and below). If the hole is at the leftmost column (σ(i)=1), then the left segment is empty, leaving only one right segment. Similarly if σ(i)=n, only left segment. So each hole contributes up to 2 horizontal segments and up to 2 vertical segments, but some may be empty. So total number of nonempty horizontal segments across all rows is exactly n + (n - number of holes at column 1 or n) = something. Let's compute: Each row i has left segment of length σ(i)-1 (maybe zero) and right segment of length n-σ(i) (maybe zero). So total number of nonempty horizontal segments across all rows is the number of rows where σ(i) ≠ 1 (left segment nonempty) plus number of rows where σ(i) ≠ n (right segment nonempty). That's (n - a) + (n - b) where a = number of rows with σ(i)=1 (i.e., column 1 hole), b = number of rows with σ(i)=n (i.e., column n hole). Since σ is a permutation, there is exactly one row with σ(i)=1 and exactly one row with σ(i)=n. So a = b = 1. So total nonempty horizontal segments = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2. Similarly, total nonempty vertical segments = 2n-2. But each rectangle can cover at most one horizontal segment per row? Actually a rectangle can cover multiple horizontal segments across rows if they align. But each rectangle can cover at most one horizontal segment per row because it cannot cross the hole. But a rectangle can cover many rows, each with a horizontal segment of the same side (left or right). So a rectangle can cover multiple horizontal segments (one per row) simultaneously, as long as they are all on the same side (left or right). So the count of horizontal segments is not a direct lower bound on number of rectangles.\n\nBetter approach: Use bipartite graph biclique partition lower bound: The edge set of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching has biclique partition number 2n-2. Provide a known proof.\n\nLet's recall known result: The bipartite dimension (or bipartite covering number) of the complement of a perfect matching is 2n-2. The bipartite dimension is the minimum number of complete bipartite subgraphs needed to partition the edges. The proof uses the fact that the adjacency matrix (the complement) has rank n over the reals? Actually the rectangle covering number is at least the Boolean rank, which is at least the rank over reals. The rank of J - I (where I is permutation matrix) is n (since J has rank 1, I has rank n). But rank bound gives lower bound of ceil(log_2 rank)? No, the Boolean rank is at least the real rank? Actually the Boolean rank (minimum number of monochromatic rectangles covering all 1's) is at least the rank over reals (since each rectangle corresponds to a rank-1 matrix). So rank of J - P is n (since J - P = J - I after permuting columns). The rank of J - I is n (since J has rank 1, I has rank n, but J - I is invertible? Let's compute: J - I has eigenvalues: n-1 (with eigenvector (1,...,1)) and -1 (with multiplicity n-1). So rank = n. So any decomposition into rank-1 matrices (i.e., rectangles) needs at least n rectangles. That's a lower bound of n, not 2n-2. So not enough.\n\nBut we need a stronger bound.\n\nConsider the following: In any partition, each rectangle can be assigned a \"type\": either left-justified (i.e., its column interval is a subset of L_i for all rows i it covers) or right-justified (subset of R_i). If a rectangle contains rows where some holes are to the left and some to the right, then its column interval must be a single column (or a single row). So we can treat those as both left- and right-justified? Actually a rectangle of width 1 can be considered both left-justified for rows with holes to the right and right-justified for rows with holes to the left. So we can treat them as either.\n\nNow, we can consider the following counting argument: For each column k (1 ≤ k ≤ n-1), consider the set of rows whose holes are to the right of column k (i.e., σ(i) > k). For those rows, column k belongs to L_i. In order to cover the cells (i,k) for those rows, we need rectangles that are left-justified and whose column interval includes column k. For each such rectangle, its rightmost column is at most the minimum σ(i)-1 among rows it covers. So each left-justified rectangle can cover column k for at most those rows whose σ(i)-1 ≥ k. But we can group many rows together as long as the smallest σ(i)-1 among them is at least k. So we could cover column k for many rows with a single rectangle that extends from column 1 to column k (or from some c to k). So we could potentially cover column k for many rows with one rectangle. So we need to argue that we need at least n-1 left-justified rectangles in total, not per column.\n\n