xactly one uncovered square and each column contains exactly one uncovered square, the uncovered squares form a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of covered squares is the complement of \\(H\\) in the board.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Matrix representation\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nJ=\\begin{pmatrix}1&\\cdots&1\\\\ \\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\ 1&\\cdots&1\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times n}\n\\]\n\nbe the all‑ones matrix, and let  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{ij})\\quad\\text{with }p_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }j=\\sigma(i),\\\\\n0 &\\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nthe permutation matrix of \\(\\sigma\\).  \nThe matrix of covered squares is\n\n\\[\nM=J-P.\n\\]\n\nThe entry \\(M_{ij}=1\\) exactly when the unit square \\((i,j)\\) is covered, and \\(M_{ij}=0\\) when it is uncovered.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Each tile is a rank‑1 matrix\n\nConsider a rectangular tile \\(T\\) that occupies the set of rows \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (both non‑empty).  \nBecause the tile must avoid all uncovered squares, we have  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(R)\\cap C=\\varnothing,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ni.e. no row \\(i\\in R\\) is paired with its forbidden column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) inside the same rectangle.\n\nThe indicator matrix of \\(T\\) is  \n\n\\[\nA_T=\\mathbf{1}_R\\mathbf{1}_C^{\\!\\top},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{1}_R\\) (resp. \\(\\mathbf{1}_C\\)) is the column vector whose entries are \\(1\\) for indices in \\(R\\) (resp. \\(C\\)) and \\(0\\) otherwise.  \nHence \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A_T)=1\\).\n\nIf the tiling uses \\(k\\) tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_k\\), the matrices \\(A_{T_1},\\dots ,A_{T_k}\\) are pairwise disjoint (their supports are disjoint) and their sum equals \\(M\\):\n\n\\[\nM=\\sum_{t=1}^{k}A_{T_t}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince rank is sub‑additive,\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le\\sum_{t=1}^{k}\\operatorname{rank}(A_{T_t})=k.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any tiling must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(M).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\)\n\nObserve that \\(P\\) is a permutation matrix, hence orthogonal: \\(P^{-1}=P^{\\!\\top}\\). Multiplying (on the right) by \\(P^{\\!\\top}\\) does not change rank:\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)=\\operatorname{rank}(MP^{\\!\\top})=\n\\operatorname{rank}(JP^{\\!\\top}-PP^{\\!\\top})=\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-I).\n\\]\n\nThe matrix \\(J-I\\) has eigenvalues  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_1=n-1\\quad\\text{(once)},\\qquad\n\\lambda_2=-1\\quad\\text{(with multiplicity }n-1\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nAll eigenvalues are non‑zero for \\(n>1\\); consequently \\(J-I\\) is invertible and  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) and (5) we obtain the **lower bound**\n\n\\[\nk\\ge n.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  A tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq i\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile covering the whole row except its diagonal cell.  \nThese \\(n\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint, they cover every non‑diagonal unit square, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  \n\nThus a tiling with \\(k=n\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Minimality\n\nThe lower bound (6) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles, while the construction of Section 5 achieves exactly \\(n\\) tiles. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(n\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\), the minimum number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
