ngular cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<i\\).  \n     For each \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     T_c=\\{(i,c):i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]  \n     a \\((n-c)\\times1\\) rectangle.  \n   - The right region \\(R\\) consists of the strictly upper‑triangular cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>i\\).  \n     For each \\(c=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     U_c=\\{(i,c):i=1,\\dots ,c-1\\},\n     \\]  \n     a \\((c-1)\\times1\\) rectangle.  \n\n   The \\(n-1\\) tiles \\(\\{T_c\\}\\) cover \\(L\\) exactly, the \\(n-1\\) tiles \\(\\{U_c\\}\\) cover \\(R\\) exactly, and the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), one per row and column.  \n   The total number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048\\).  \n\nSince the lower bound equals the construction, the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).  "
