).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\ni_j=\\min S_j .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, \\(S_j\\neq\\varnothing\\) for every \\(j\\) and \\(i_j\\) is well defined.  \nThe cell \\((i_j,j)\\) is the **topmost** cell of \\(L\\) in column \\(j\\).\n\nConsider any rectangle \\(R\\) used in a tiling.  \nIf \\(R\\) contains a cell of column \\(j\\), then all rows of \\(R\\) must belong to \\(S_j\\); otherwise a row \\(k\\notin S_j\\) would contribute a hole \\((k,p(k))\\) with \\(p(k)\\le j\\) lying inside the column interval of \\(R\\), contradicting the rule that a tile may not contain a hole.  \nConsequently the row interval of \\(R\\) is a **contiguous subset of \\(S_j\\)** and must start at a row \\(\\ge i_j\\).  \n\nIf a rectangle covers cells of two different columns \\(j\\) and \\(j'\\) with \\(i_j\\neq i_{j'}\\), its row interval would have to start at two different rows, which is impossible.  \nThus a single rectangle can cover cells of several columns only when those columns share the same value of \\(i_j\\).\n\nNow observe that the values \\(\\{i_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) are exactly the rows whose uncovered square is **not** in column 1.  \nIndeed, for a row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) we have \\(j=p(i)-1\\) and then \\(i_j=i\\); conversely, if \\(p(i)=1\\) then \\(i\\) never appears as an \\(i_j\\).  \nHence the set \\(\\{i_j\\}\\) has cardinality \\(n-1\\).\n\nSince each distinct value of \\(i_j\\) forces a different rectangle, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover the whole left region \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the right region  \n\nThe right region is symmetric.  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_j=\\{\\,i\\in[n]\\mid p(i)<j\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nt_j=\\max T_j .\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\((t_j,j)\\) is the **bottommost** cell of \\(R\\) in column \\(j\\).  \nExactly the same argument as for the left region shows that a rectangle covering column \\(j\