cell in L and a cell in R, then the column interval contains p(i) for some row i and row interval contains row i, thus hole is inside tile, contradiction. This is correct.\n\nLemma 2: They define c_i = (i, p(i)-1) for rows with p(i)>1. Exactly one row has p(i)=1, so there are n-1 distinct cells c_i. Claim: No rectangle Q subset L can contain two distinct cells c_i and c_i'. They prove by assuming i < i' and analyzing column intervals. They consider a = p(i)-1, a' = p(i')-1. If a <= a', then column a' is in interval, row i is in interval, so cell (i, a') is in Q. Since a' >= a+1 = p(i), column a' is >= p(i), i.e., to the right of the hole's column for row i. So (i, a') is not in L, contradiction. Similarly if a > a', symmetric argument. This is correct.\n\nThus each rectangle covering L can contain at most one c_i. So at least n-1 rectangles needed to cover L. This is correct.\n\nLemma 3: symmetric for R.\n\nThus lower bound 2n-2.\n\nConstruction: identity permutation. L = {j < i}, R = {i < j}. Cover L with vertical strips L_j = {(i,j) | j+1 <= i <= n}. That's correct. Cover R with horizontal strips R_i = {(i,j) | i+1 <= j <= n}. That's correct. They are disjoint and cover all L and R. So total 2n-2 rectangles.\n\nThus solution is correct, reasoning valid, presentation seems fine. However, we need to check if any \"construction claim\" is missing concrete details. The construction is explicit: they define each rectangle L_j and R_i with formulas. That's concrete. So it's Category C (valid method). So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow check for any potential justification gaps: The solution might have some minor imprecisions: In Lemma 2, they say \"Since a' >= a+1 = p(i)\". Actually a = p(i)-1, so a+1 = p(i). So a' >= a+1 means a' >= p(i). That's correct. Then they say \"column a' is strictly to the right of the left region of row i (which ends at column p(i)-1 = a)\". So (i, a') not in L. That's correct. The argument is fine.\n\nPotential minor gap: They assume that the rectangle Q contains both cells c_i and c_i', but they didn't explicitly argue that the rectangle's column interval includes both a and a' and row interval includes both i and i'. That's obvious from rectangle definition. So fine.\n\nPotential minor gap: In Lemma 1, they say \"If T contained a cell (i,j) in L and a cell (i',j') in R, then the column interval of T would contain column p(i) (because j < p(i) < j')\". However, they need to ensure that p(i) is between j and j', but j' may be from a different row i', not necessarily same row i. But they argue column interval includes p(i) because j < p(i) < j'. That's correct because j < p(i) (since (i,j) in L means j < p(i)) and j' > p(i') (since (i',j') in R means j' > p(i')). But does it guarantee p(i) < j'? Actually j' > p(i'), not necessarily > p(i). But they claim column interval contains p(i) because j < p(i) < j'. Wait, need to check: Suppose we have a tile covering cells (i,j) with j < p(i) and (i',j') with j' > p(i'). The column interval of the tile is from min(j, j') to max(j, j'). Since j < p(i) and j' > p(i'), we cannot guarantee that p(i) is between j and j' unless we know p(i) <= j' and p(i) >= j. Actually we know j < p(i). For p(i) < j', we need to know that j' > p(i). But we only know j' > p(i'). p(i') could be less than p(i) or greater. So it's not guaranteed that p(i) < j'. However, we could argue that the column interval includes all columns between j and j', which includes p(i) if p(i) lies between them. But we don't know that. So Lemma 1's argument might be flawed: they need to show that any rectangle containing a cell left of its row's hole and a cell right of some (maybe different) row's hole must contain some hole. The argument as given might be insufficient: they need to consider the row intervals as well. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have a rectangle T = [r1, r2] x [c1, c2] (with rows r1 <= r2, columns c1 <= c2). Suppose T contains a cell (i,j) in L, i.e., j < p(i). So j in [c1, c2] and i in [r1, r2]. Also T contains a cell (i',j') in R, i.e., j' > p(i'). So j' in [c1, c2] and i' in [r1, r2].\n\nWe need to show that there exists some row k such that p(k) is in [c1, c2] and k in [r1, r2], i.e., the tile contains the hole (k, p(k)). The claim in Lemma 1 says: \"the column interval of T would contain the column p(i) (because j < p(i) < j'), and the row interval would contain the row i; consequently (i,p(i)) in T\". But as noted, we cannot guarantee p(i) < j' unless p(i) <= j' (i.e., p(i) <= j'). But we only know j' > p(i'), not p(i). So the argument is flawed.\n\nBut perhaps they meant to consider the same row i for both cells: i.e., if a tile contains a cell left of the hole in row i and a cell right of the hole in the same row i, then it must contain the hole. However, they didn't specify that the two cells are in the same row. They said \"If T contained a cell (i,j) in L and a cell (i',j') in R\". That could be different rows. If they are same row, then indeed j < p(i) < j', so column interval includes p(i), row interval includes i, so hole is inside. But if i' != i, we need a different argument. However, the lemma might be salvageable: If T contains a cell left of its row's hole (i.e., in L) and also contains a cell right of some hole (maybe different row), then consider the row i (the row of the left cell). Since T also contains a cell (i',j') with j' > p(i'), but we don't know about p(i). However, the column interval includes both j and j', and j < p(i). If j' > p(i), then p(i) is inside interval. But if j' <= p(i), then p(i) may be > j', but we still have j < p(i). So p(i) could be > j' but still within interval if j' is the max column. Actually column interval is from min(j, j') to max(j, j'). Since j < p(i), we have min(j, j') <= j < p(i). For p(i) to be inside interval, we need p(i) <= max(j, j'). Since max(j, j') = max(j, j'), which is at least j' (if j' > j) else j. If j' > j, then max = j', so need p(i) <= j'. If j' <= p(i), then p(i) > max, not