ow4: column1. R = cells right of holes: row1: none; row2: columns2-4; row3: column4; row4: columns3-4.\n\nNow consider rectangle containing (1,1) in L (row1, col1) and (2,4) in R (row2, col4). Row interval = rows1-2, column interval = cols1-4. Does rectangle contain any hole? It includes (1,4) (hole), (2,1) (hole). Indeed contains holes. So okay.\n\nConsider rectangle containing (3,2) in L (row3, col2) and (4,4) in R (row4, col4). Row interval rows3-4, column interval cols2-4. Holes in rows3-4: (3,3) and (4,2). Are they inside rectangle? (3,3) is row3, col3, which is inside rows3-4, cols2-4, so yes. (4,2) is row4, col2, also inside rectangle. So rectangle contains holes.\n\nTry to find a rectangle that contains left cell from row i and right cell from row i' but avoids holes. Suppose we pick left cell (i,j) where j is far left, and right cell (i',j') where j' is far right, but maybe the holes in those rows are outside the column interval. For that to happen, we need p(i) > j' (so hole in row i is to the right of column interval), and p(i') < j (hole in row i' is to the left of column interval). That would avoid holes in those rows. But there might be other rows in between that have holes inside interval. Could we avoid those too? Possibly if the permutation is such that p(k) for all rows k between i and i' are either all less than j or all greater than j'. But that seems impossible because p(k) are a permutation of [n]; they cannot all be less than j (which is small) or all greater than j' (which is large) if the interval [j, j'] is not covering the whole [1,n]. But maybe the rows between i and i' are few, and p(k) values are outside the interval.\n\nLet's try to construct a counterexample: n=5. Permutation p: row1: p(1)=5, row2: p(2)=1, row3: p(3)=4, row4: p(4)=2, row5: p(5)=3. So holes: (1,5), (2,1), (3,4), (4,2), (5,3). L: row1: cols1-4; row2: none; row3: cols1-3; row4: col1; row5: cols1-2. R: row1: none; row2: cols2-5; row3: col5; row4: cols3-5; row5: col4-5.\n\nNow pick left cell (row3, col1) in L (since p(3)=4, left region includes col1-3). Pick right cell (row4, col5) in R (since p(4)=2, right region includes cols3-5). So rectangle rows3-4, cols1-5 includes all columns. Contains holes: (3,4) and (4,2). So includes holes.\n\nTry left cell (row4, col1) in L, right cell (row2, col5) in R. Row interval rows2-4, column interval cols1-5 includes all holes.\n\nIt seems difficult to avoid holes because the rectangle covering left and right cells tends to be large enough to include holes. But is it always true? Let's try to prove the lemma: Suppose rectangle T contains a left cell (i,j) with j < p(i) and a right cell (i',j') with j' > p(i'). Without loss of generality, assume i <= i' (if not, swap). Then the row interval is [i,i']. The column interval is [min(j,j'), max(j,j')]. Since j < p(i) and j' > p(i'), we have min(j,j') <= j < p(i) and max(j,j') >= j' > p(i'). So column interval contains p(i) if p(i) <= max(j,j') and p(i) >= min(j,j'). We know p(i) > j, but we don't know if p(i) <= max(j,j'). However, max(j,j') >= j' > p(i'), but p(i) could be > j' (i.e., p(i) > max). If p(i) > max(j,j'), then p(i) > j' and p(i) > j. So p(i) > max. Similarly, p(i') < min(j,j')? Actually p(i') < j' but we don't know relation to j. Could be p(i') < j (if j is large) or p(i') > j (if j is small). So it's possible that p(i) > max and p(i') < min. In that case, the column interval does not contain either p(i) or p(i'), but maybe contains some p(k) for k between i and i'? Let's examine.\n\nWe have a permutation p: rows i..i' each have a column p(k). The column interval [min(j,j'), max(j,j')] is some interval. We have j < p(i) and j' > p(i'). So p(i) is to the right of j, p(i') is to the left of j'. So p(i) > j and p(i') < j'. So the interval [j, j'] contains p(i')? Actually p(i') < j', but could be > j or < j. Similarly p(i) > j, but could be > j' or < j'.\n\nIf p(i) > j' (i.e., p(i) > max), then p(i) > j' > p(i'), so p(i) > j' > p(i'). So p(i')