n R. So we partition the board into two regions L and R. Then they argue that each rectangle covering L can contain at most one of the rightmost cells c_i of each row (except the row where p(i)=1). That seems plausible: each c_i is the cell just left of the hole in that row. If a rectangle in L contains two such cells from different rows, then the rectangle's column interval includes both columns a and a', and row interval includes both rows i and i'. Then they argue that the rectangle would contain a cell (i,a') or (i',a) which would be to the right of the hole in that row, thus not in L. That seems correct. So each rectangle can contain at most one such cell. Since there are n-1 such cells, need at least n-1 rectangles to cover L. Similarly for R. So total at least 2n-2. That seems correct.\n\nConstruction: identity permutation, holes on diagonal. L = lower triangle (strictly below diagonal). They cover each column j (1 <= j <= n-1) with vertical strip covering rows j+1 to n (i.e., cells below diagonal in column j). That's a rectangle of width 1, height n-j. These are disjoint and cover all cells in L. That's n-1 rectangles. Similarly, R = upper triangle (strictly above diagonal). For each row i (1 <= i <= n-1), they cover columns i+1 to n (horizontal strip). That's n-1 rectangles. So total 2n-2 rectangles. So indeed they achieve lower bound.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nNow check reasoning validity: They used definitions, case analysis, counting argument, proof by contradiction (in Lemma 1). They used a combinatorial argument to bound number of rectangles. All methods are valid. They also used a construction with explicit rectangles. So reasoning uses valid methods.\n\nNow check presentation: Are there any gaps? Let's examine the solution for any missing details or errors.\n\nThe solution is fairly thorough. However, we need to check if any claim is false or missing justification. Let's go through each lemma.\n\nLemma 1: They argue that if a tile contains a cell in L and a cell in R, then the column interval contains p(i) for some row i and row interval contains row i, thus hole is inside tile, contradiction. This is correct.\n\nLemma 2: They define c_i = (i, p(i)-1) for rows with p(i)>1. Exactly one row has p(i)=1, so there are n-1 distinct cells c_i. Claim: No rectangle Q subset L can contain two distinct cells c_i and c_i'. They prove by assuming i < i' and analyzing column intervals. They consider a = p(i)-1, a' = p(i')-1. If a <= a', then column a' is in interval, row i is in interval, so cell (i, a') is in Q. Since a' >= a+1 = p(i), column a' is >= p(i), i.e., to the right of the hole's column for row i. So (i, a') is not in L, contradiction. Similarly if a > a', symmetric argument. This is correct.\n\nThus each rectangle covering L can contain at most one c_i. So at least n-1 rectangles needed to cover L. This is correct.\n\nLemma 3: symmetric for R.\n\nThus lower bound 2n-2.\n\nConstruction: identity permutation. L = {j < i}, R = {i < j}. Cover L with vertical strips L_j = {(i,j) | j+1 <= i <= n}. That's correct. Cover R with horizontal strips R_i = {(i,j) | i+1 <= j <= n}. That's correct. They are disjoint and cover all L and R. So total 2n-2 rectangles.\n\nThus solution is correct, reasoning valid, presentation seems fine. However, we need to check if any \"construction claim\" is missing concrete details. The construction is explicit: they define each rectangle L_j and R_i with formulas. That's concrete. So it's Category C (valid method). So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow check for any potential justification gaps: The solution might have some minor imprecisions: In Lemma 2, they say \"Since a' >= a+1 = p(i)\". Actually a = p(i)-1, so a+1 = p(i). So a' >= a+1 means a' >= p(i). That's correct. Then they say \"column a' is strictly to the right of the left region of row i (which ends at column p(i)-1 = a)\". So (i, a') not in L. That's correct. The argument is fine.\n\nPotential minor gap: They assume that the rectangle Q