2]. Also T contains a cell (i',j') in R, i.e., j' > p(i'). So j' in [c1, c2] and i' in [r1, r2].\n\nWe need to show that there exists some row k such that p(k) is in [c1, c2] and k in [r1, r2], i.e., the tile contains the hole (k, p(k)). The claim in Lemma 1 says: \"the column interval of T would contain the column p(i) (because j < p(i) < j'), and the row interval would contain the row i; consequently (i,p(i)) in T\". But as noted, we cannot guarantee p(i) < j' unless p(i) <= j' (i.e., p(i) <= j'). But we only know j' > p(i'), not p(i). So the argument is flawed.\n\nBut perhaps they meant to consider the same row i for both cells: i.e., if a tile contains a cell left of the hole in row i and a cell right of the hole in the same row i, then it must contain the hole. However, they didn't specify that the two cells are in the same row. They said \"If T contained a cell (i,j) in L and a cell (i',j') in R\". That could be different rows. If they are same row, then indeed j < p(i) < j', so column interval includes p(i), row interval includes i, so hole is inside. But if i' != i, we need a different argument. However, the lemma might be salvageable: If T contains a cell left of its row's hole (i.e., in L) and also contains a cell right of some hole (maybe different row), then consider the row i (the row of the left cell). Since T also contains a cell (i',j') with j' > p(i'), but we don't know about p(i). However, the column interval includes both j and j', and j < p(i). If j' > p(i), then p(i) is inside interval. But if j' <= p(i), then p(i) may be > j', but we still have j < p(i). So p(i) could be > j' but still within interval if j' is the max column. Actually column interval is from min(j, j') to max(j, j'). Since j < p(i), we have min(j, j') <= j < p(i). For p(i) to be inside interval, we need p(i) <= max(j, j'). Since max(j, j') = max(j, j'), which is at least j' (if j' > j) else j. If j' > j, then max = j', so need p(i) <= j'. If j' <= p(i), then p(i) > max, not inside. So the argument fails if j' <= p(i). But we only know j' > p(i'), not p(i). So it's possible that j' <= p(i). For example, suppose p(i) = 10, p(i') = 5, j = 3 (<10), j' = 6 (>5). Then j' = 6 < p(i) = 10, so p(i) not in column interval [3,6]. So the tile may not contain (i,10). However, the tile contains (i',6) which is right of hole in row i', but not necessarily containing any hole. But perhaps the tile contains the hole in row i'? Let's check: For row i', hole is at column p(i') = 5. The tile contains (i',6). Since column interval includes 6, and row interval includes i', does it contain (i',5)? The column interval is [3,6], so includes 5. So (i',5) is inside tile. So tile contains the hole (i',5). Indeed, if tile contains a cell right of the hole in row i', then column interval includes p(i')? Actually we have j' > p(i'), so p(i') < j', and column interval includes all columns between j and j'. Since j < p(i')? Not necessarily. But we need to check if p(i') is between j and j'. Since j <= j' (if j < j'), and p(i') < j', but we don't know if p(i') >= j. If j <= p(i'), then p(i') is inside column interval. If j > p(i'), then p(i') is less than min column, not inside. But we have j < p(i) (some other row's hole). Could be j > p(i') as well. So we cannot guarantee that p(i') is inside interval either.\n\nThus Lemma 1's argument is not fully rigorous as stated. However, the conclusion that each tile must be wholly in L or wholly in R might still be true, but the provided reasoning might be incomplete or flawed. Let's examine the problem more thoroughly: Is it true that any rectangle covering squares on both sides of holes must contain a hole? Let's think about the geometry: The holes are at positions (i, p(i)). L is left of each hole in its row, R is right of each hole. The shape of L is a region that is left of the \"staircase\" defined by the permutation. R is right of the staircase. The region L is not convex; it's a \"Young diagram\" shape. Similarly R is the complement shape (excluding holes). The claim is that any axis-aligned rectangle that does not contain any hole must lie entirely in L or entirely in R. Is this true? Let's test with a small example: n=3. Suppose permutation p = (2,3,1). So holes: (1,2), (2,3), (3,1). L = cells left of each hole: row1: column1; row2: columns1,2; row3: none (since p(3)=1, left region empty). So L = {(1,1), (2,1), (2,2)}. R = cells right of each hole: row1: columns3; row2: none; row3: columns2,3. So R = {(1,3), (3,2), (3,3)}. The holes are (1,2), (2,3), (3,1). Now consider a rectangle covering (1,1) (in L) and (1,3) (in R). That's a rectangle covering row1 columns1 to3. That rectangle includes (1,2) which is a hole. So indeed any rectangle containing cells from both L and R in the same row will contain the hole. But what about rectangle covering (2,1) (L) and (3,2) (R)? That's rows2-3, columns1-2. Does it contain any hole? Holes: (2,3) is at column3, not in rectangle; (3,1) is at column1, row3, column1. The rectangle includes (3,1) because row3, column1 is within rows2-3, columns1-2. So it contains a hole. Indeed, rectangle includes (3,1). So that example supports lemma.\n\nBut is there any rectangle that can include a left cell from one row and a right cell from another row without covering a hole? Let's try to find a counterexample. The shape L is \"southwest\" of the permutation graph, R is \"northeast\". The graph of permutation is a monotone path (since it's a permutation, not necessarily monotone). Actually the permutation graph can be any arrangement of points (i,p(i)). The region L is set of points (i,j) with j < p(i). The region R is points with j > p(i). The shape L is like a Young diagram but not necessarily monotone in columns? Let's think: For each row i, L includes columns 1 to p(i)-1. So L is a union of left-justified intervals per row. Similarly, R is union of right-justified intervals per row. The shape L is \"staircase\" but can have irregular shape because p(i) can vary arbitrarily. However, L is a \"Ferrers diagram\" shape if we sort rows by p(i) decreasing? Actually if we reorder rows, it's a Ferrers shape. But as is, L may have \"holes\" in columns: some columns may have L cells in some rows but not others.\n\nNow consider a rectangle that includes cells (i,j) with j < p(i) and (i',j') with j' > p(i'). The rectangle's column interval is [min(j,j'), max(j,j')]. It may contain p(i) if p(i) lies between j and j'. But if p(i) > max(j,j'), then p(i) not inside. Similarly, p(i') may be inside if p(i') lies between j and j'. But we cannot guarantee. However, the rectangle also includes all rows between i and i'. So maybe there is some row k in that interval such that p(k) lies between j and j'? Because the permutation graph has one point per row, and the columns p(k) vary. The rectangle's column interval covers a range of columns. The rectangle's row interval covers a range of rows. So the rectangle includes all points (k, p(k)) for k in row interval. Some of those p(k) might be inside column interval. If any such point exists, the rectangle contains a hole. So the lemma is essentially that any rectangle that contains a left cell and a right cell must also contain some hole. This is plausible because the rectangle's row interval includes the rows of both cells; the column interval includes the columns of both cells. Since the permutation points are one per row, and the columns p(k) are a permutation of [n], there must be at least one p(k) between the min and max of the two columns. Actually, we need to prove that for any two rows i and i', with columns j < p(i) and j' > p(i'), the interval of columns [j, j'] must contain some p(k) for some k between i and i'. This is not guaranteed for arbitrary permutation. Let's test with a counterexample.\n\nTake n=4. Permutation p: p(1)=4, p(2)=1, p(3)=3, p(4)=2. So holes: (1,4), (2,1), (3,3), (4,2). L = cells left of holes: row1: columns1-3; row2: none (since p(2)=1); row3: columns1-2; row4: