' < p(i). So p(i') is left of j', p(i) is right of j'. So the interval [j,j'] contains p(i')? No, because p(i') < j, maybe? Actually if p(i') < j, then p(i') is left of interval. If p(i') > j, then p(i') is inside interval. So we need to consider possibilities.\n\nThus the lemma's statement that any rectangle containing a left cell and a right cell must contain a hole is not trivial. However, the solution's proof of Lemma 1 is flawed as we observed: they assume the same row i for both left and right cells, which is not guaranteed. But perhaps they implicitly assume that the rectangle contains a left cell and a right cell from the same row. But they didn't state that. However, maybe the lemma can be salvaged: If a rectangle contains any left cell (i,j) and any right cell (i',j'), then consider the row i of the left cell. Since the rectangle also contains a right cell, its column interval includes some column > p(i') but not necessarily > p(i). However, the rectangle also contains the entire column interval, which includes all columns between j and j'. Since j < p(i), if j' >= p(i), then p(i) is inside interval. If j' < p(i), then p(i) > j', but then p(i) > max(j,j'), so p(i) not inside. But then consider row i' of the right cell: we have j' > p(i'). Since j <= p(i)-1, if j <= p(i')? Not sure.\n\nLet's think more systematically: Suppose rectangle T = [r1, r2] x [c1, c2] with r1 <= r2, c1 <= c2. It contains a left cell (i,j) with j < p(i). So i in [r1, r2] and j in [c1, c2]. It also contains a right cell (i',j') with j' > p(i'). So i' in [r1, r2] and j' in [c1, c2].\n\nWe need to show there exists some k in [r1, r2] such that p(k) in [c1, c2]. Because then (k, p(k)) is a hole inside T.\n\nNow consider the set S = {p(k) : k in [r1, r2]}. Since p is a permutation of [n], S is a subset of [n] of size (r2 - r1 + 1). The interval [c1, c2] is a set of columns. We need to show S ∩ [c1, c2] ≠ ∅. Suppose for contradiction that S ∩ [c1, c2] = ∅, i.e., all p(k) are either < c1 or