ach row, \\(R\\) consists of the squares strictly to the right, and  \n\n\\[\nL\\cup R=\\bigl([n]\\times[n]\\bigr)\\setminus\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\},\\qquad \nL\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (tiles stay in one region)\n\nLet \\(T\\) be an axis‑parallel rectangle that contains no uncovered square.  \nIf \\(T\\) contained a cell \\((i,j)\\in L\\) (so \\(j<p(i)\\)) and a cell \\((i',j')\\in R\\) (so \\(j'>p(i')\\)), then the column interval of \\(T\\) would contain the column \\(p(i)\\) (because \\(j<p(i)<j'\\)), and the row interval would contain the row \\(i\\); consequently \\((i,p(i))\\in T\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(T\\) avoids uncovered squares.  \nHence every tile is contained wholly in \\(L\\) or wholly in \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (lower bound for \\(L\\))\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) define the *rightmost* cell of that row in \\(L\\),\n\n\\[\nc_i=(i,p(i)-1).\n\\]\n\nExactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\); therefore the set \\(\\{c_i\\}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) distinct cells, each lying in a different row.\n\n*Claim.* No rectangle \\(Q\\subseteq L\\) can contain two distinct cells \\(c_i\\) and \\(c_{i'}\\).\n\n*Proof of claim.* Assume \\(i<i'\\) and that \\(Q\\) contains \\(c_i=(i,a)\\) and \\(c_{i'}=(i',a')\\) where \\(a=p(i)-1,\\;a'=p(i')-1\\).  \nBecause \\(Q\\) contains both cells, its column interval contains both \\(a\\) and \\(a'\\), and its row interval contains both \\(i\\) and \\(i'\\).\n\nIf \\(a\\le a'\\), then column \\(a'\\) belongs to the column interval and row \\(i\\) belongs to the row interval, so the cell \\((i,a')\\) lies in \\(Q\\).  \nSince \\(a'\\ge a+1=p(i)\\), column \\(a'\\) is strictly to the right of the left region of row \\(i\\) (which ends at column \\(p(i)-1=a\\)). Hence \\((i,a')\\notin L\\), contradicting \\(Q\\subseteq L\\).\n\nIf \\(a>a'\\), the symmetric argument using the cell \\((i',a)\\) yields the same contradiction. ∎\n\nThus each rectangle covering \\(L\\) can contain at most one of the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(c_i\\). Consequently any tiling of \\(L\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (lower bound for \\(R\\))\n\nThe argument of Lemma 2 is symmetric.  \nFor each row with \\(p(i)<n\\) consider the *leftmost* cell of that row in \\(R\\),\n\n\\[\nd_i=(i,p(i)+1).\n\\]\n\nThere are again \\(n-1\\) such cells, each in a distinct row, and a rectangle contained in \\(R\\) cannot contain two of them. Hence any tiling of \\(R\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Consequence\n\nAny tiling of the whole board consists of a tiling of \\(L\\) together with a tiling of \\(R\\); by Lemmas 2 and 3 it uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Covering \\(L\\).* For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nL_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_j\\) is a \\(1\\)-wide strip covering exactly the cells of column \\(j\\) that lie below the diagonal.  \nThe strips \\(\\{L_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union equals \\(L\\).\n\n*Covering \\(R\\).* For each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R_i\\) is a \\(1\\)-high strip covering exactly the cells of row \\(i\\) that lie above the diagonal.  \nThe strips \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union equals \\(R\\).\n\nEvery cell of \\(L\\) lies in some \\(L_j\\), every cell of \\(R\\) lies in some \\(R_i\\), and no rectangle contains a diagonal cell.  \nHence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}=\\{L_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{R_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nis a tiling of the board with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangular tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Minimality\n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\" for complete rigor. The claim is mathematically correct and the reasoning is sound, but lacks an explicit justification step. This is a presentation issue, not a mathematical error.\n\n**WRONG Classification (don't do 