ch column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) consider the set  \n\n\\[\nC_j:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the hole in row \\(i\\) lies at column \\(i\\), the column \\(j\\) is free exactly\nin the rows \\(j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\); these rows are consecutive.  Hence \\(C_j\\) is a\nsingle vertical rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).  The \\(n-1\\) rectangles\n\\(\\{C_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\) cover all squares left of the diagonal.\n\n*Right side \\(\\mathcal R\\).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) consider  \n\n\\[\nD_j:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow column \\(j\\) is free exactly in the rows \\(1,2,\\dots ,j-1\\), which again form a\ncontiguous block.  Thus each \\(D_j\\) is a vertical rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\).\nThe \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{D_j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\) cover all squares right of the\ndiagonal.\n\nThe two families are disjoint, together they cover every unit square except the\n\\(n\\) diagonal holes, and they consist of exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.\nHence a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction shows that the lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is attainable; therefore it\nis the minimum possible number of tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Numerical answer  \n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
