res. n - r = 0.\n\nThus region above holes is indeed the same triangular region independent of permutation. Similarly region below holes is also triangular region (mirror). Indeed, region below holes: squares (r,c) where r > i_c. For each row r, number of columns where i_c < r = r - 1. So row r has r-1 squares below holes. So the shape is symmetric.\n\nThus the region above holes is a fixed shape: a right triangle of squares above the main diagonal (strictly above). Similarly below region is the triangle below main diagonal. So the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition each triangle is indeed n-1 (since each row has decreasing lengths). But perhaps we can partition a triangle shape with fewer than n-1 rectangles? Let's examine.\n\nFor n=4, triangle shape above diagonal has rows lengths 3,2,1. Minimal number of rectangles needed to partition that shape: we found it's 3 (n-1). Could we do with 2? Let's try: rectangle covering rows1-2 columns2-4? That's rows1-2 columns2-4 includes squares (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). That's 6 squares. The triangle above diagonal has squares (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4),(3,4). So rectangle rows1-2 columns2-4 includes (2,2) which is not part of the triangle (since (2,2) is a hole). So not allowed. Could we use rectangle rows1-3 columns4-4 (i.e., column4 rows1-3)? That includes (2,4) and (3,4) and (1,4). That's 3 squares. Then remaining squares: (1,2),(1,3),(2,3). Could we cover those with one rectangle? rectangle rows1-2 columns2-3 includes (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3). (2,2) is a hole, not allowed. So can't. So at least 3 rectangles needed.\n\nThus the triangle shape requires n-1 rectangles. This is a known combinatorial fact: the Ferrers diagram of shape (n-1,n-2,...,1) has rectangle partition number equal to n-1. Because each rectangle can cover at most one cell in the first row (since the first row has length n-1, but any rectangle covering any cell in the first row must have its column interval within the row's column