ares above holes: column2 rows1, column3 rows1-2. Could we combine them into a rectangle covering rows1-1 (just row1) and columns2-3? That would be rectangle covering squares (1,2),(1,3). That's okay. That would replace two separate rectangles (one covering (1,2) and one covering (1,3) maybe). Actually, vertical approach would have column2 above hole: (1,2) as a vertical rectangle of size 1x1. Column3 above hole: (1,3),(2,3) as a vertical rectangle of size 2x1. So we have two rectangles: one covering (1,2) and one covering (1,3),(2,3). Could we combine them into a single rectangle covering rows1-2, columns2-3? That rectangle includes squares (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3). But (2,2) is a hole. So can't. Could we combine (1,2) and (1,3) into a horizontal rectangle covering row1, columns2-3? That rectangle includes (1,2),(1,3) only, which are both not holes. That's allowed. So we can replace two rectangles (vertical ones) with one horizontal rectangle covering (1,2),(1,3). That reduces count by 1. Similarly, we could combine some other rectangles.\n\nLet's attempt to construct a covering with 5 rectangles for identity permutation n=4.\n\nWe have holes at (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4). The complement squares: we can think of the board as 4x4 with diagonal holes.\n\nOne possible covering: Use horizontal rectangles for each row except maybe combine some rows.\n\nRow1: squares (1,2),(1,3),(1,4). That's a horizontal rectangle covering columns2-4, row1. That's one rectangle.\n\nRow2: squares (2,1),(2,3),(2,4). Could we cover them with two rectangles? (2,1) is left of hole, (2,3),(2,4) are right of hole. Could we combine (2,3),(2,4) as a horizontal rectangle covering columns3-4, row2. That's one rectangle. (2,1) is a single square; could combine with something else across rows? Possibly combine (2,1) with (3,1) and (4,1) as a vertical rectangle covering column1 rows2-4? But column1 has hole at (1,1). So squares (2,1),(3,1),(4,1) are below the hole, can be covered by a vertical rectangle 