red squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe covered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nL:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad\nR:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i,\n\\]\nand \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Separation of tiles)\n\nLet \\(T\\) be an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior consists only of covered squares and which contains squares from at least two distinct rows. Then either \\(T\\subseteq L\\) or \\(T\\subseteq R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose \\(T\\) meets rows \\(i\\neq k\\). Let \\(a=\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(b=\\sigma(k)\\). Without loss of generality \\(a<b\\).  \nIf a column of \\(T\\) lay between \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), then that column would contain the hole \\((i,a)\\) (or \\((k,b)\\)), contradicting that \\(T\\) consists only of covered squares. Hence every column of \\(T\\) is either \\(\\le a-1\\) (left of both holes) or \\(\\ge b+1\\) (right of both holes). Consequently all cells of \\(T\\) lie entirely in the left parts of the rows it meets (i.e. in \\(L\\)) or entirely in the right parts (i.e. in \\(R\\)). ∎\n\nThus the family of tiles splits into two subfamilies: those covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Lower bound for the left side)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_i:=(i,\\sigma(i)-1),\n\\]\n\nthe rightmost cell of \\(L_i\\). There are exactly \\(n-1\\) such cells (the unique row with \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) has no cell of this form). Any rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can contain at most one of the cells \\(r_i\\). Consequently any covering of \\(L\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(T\\subseteq L\\) contains two distinct cells \\(r_i\\) and \\(r_