                    "reasoning": "We have a problem: Determine the minimum number of tiles needed to cover a 2025x2025 grid with axis-aligned rectangular tiles (with sides on grid lines) such that each row and each column has exactly one unit square not covered (i.e., a hole). Tiles cannot overlap and each unit square can be covered at most once. So we need to find minimal number of tiles.\n\nThe solution provided: They define n=2025, define sigma(i) as column of uncovered square in row i, which must be a permutation. Then they define left side L (cells left of holes) and right side R (cells right of holes). They prove that any rectangle covering cells from multiple rows must be entirely in L or entirely in R (Lemma 1). Then they prove lower bound: covering L requires at least n-1 rectangles (Lemma 2) using rightmost cells of each L_i (r_i). Similarly covering R requires at least n-1 rectangles (Lemma 3). So total at least 2n-2 = 4048 tiles.\n\nThen they construct a tiling achieving this bound: choose sigma(i)=i (holes on diagonal). Then L = cells below diagonal (j < i) and R = cells above diagonal (j > i). They place horizontal tiles H_i covering row i to the right of diagonal (i.e., cells (i, i+1) to (i, n)), each is a 1 x (n-i) tile. And vertical tiles V_j covering column j below diagonal (cells (j+1 to n, j)), each is (n-j) x 1 tile. That's total (n-1) horizontal + (n-1) vertical = 2n-2 tiles. They argue they are disjoint and cover all cells except diagonal holes.\n\nThus answer 4048.\n\nWe need to verify correctness, reasoning validity, and presentation issues.\n\nFirst, check answer correctness: The answer is 4048. Is that correct? Let's think about the problem. The solution seems plausible. But we need to verify if the lower bound is correct and the construction works. Let's think about the problem in more depth.\n\nWe have a n x n grid. We need to place rectangular tiles (axis-aligned) covering cells, each cell covered at most once (i.e., tiles are disjoint). Each row and each column must have exactly one uncovered unit square. So we have n uncovered squares, one per row and column, thus they form a permutation matrix. So uncovered squares are at positions (i, sigma(i)). So far correct.\n\nWe need to tile the rest of the cells with rectangles. Each rectangle must be axis-aligned, covering a set of cells that form a rectangle (contiguous rows and columns). Tiles cannot overlap, but they can be adjacent.\n\nGoal: minimize number of rectangles. Equivalent to partitioning the set of covered cells into rectangles.\n\nWe need to find minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix.\n\nThe solution claims lower bound 2n-2. Is that minimal? Let's test small n to see if it's plausible. For n=1: trivial? Actually n=1 grid has one cell. Must have exactly one uncovered cell per row and column, so the only cell must be uncovered. Then no tiles needed. 2n-2 = 0, correct.\n\nFor n=2: need one uncovered per row and column, so uncovered squares are either (1,1) and (2,2) (identity), or (1,2) and (2,1) (swap). Let's test identity case: holes at (1,1) and (2,2). Then cells to cover: (1,2) and (2,1). They are not adjacent in a rectangle (they are diagonal). So each must be a separate 1x1 tile? Actually tiles can be any rectangle, including 1x1. So need at least 2 tiles. 2n-2 = 2. So matches.\n\nIf we choose swap case: holes at (1,2) and (2,1). Then cells to cover: (1,1) and (2,2). Also diagonal, need 2 tiles. So minimal is 2. So lower bound matches.\n\nFor n=3: holes at (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). Cells to cover: all off-diagonal cells. The solution's construction uses horizontal tiles for each row i covering columns > i: row1: (1,2),(1,3) as a 1x2 tile; row2: (2,3) as 1x1 tile; row3: none. And vertical tiles for each column j covering rows > j: column1: (2,1),(3,1) as 2x1 tile; column2: (3,2) as 1x1 tile; column3: none. So total tiles: (n-1)+(n-1)=4 tiles. 2n-2 = 4. Is that minimal? Could we do with fewer