s column interval includes sigma(k) (since it includes sigma(k)-1 and sigma(i)-1, and all columns between). So column sigma(k) is included. In row i, column sigma(k) may be left or right of hole? Since sigma(k) < sigma(i), column sigma(k) is left of sigma(i) (hole in row i). So (i, sigma(k)) is a cell left of hole in row i, thus belongs to L. However, the rectangle includes column sigma(k) for all rows between i and k (including row i). But does that cause a hole? The hole in row k is at column sigma(k). The rectangle includes column sigma(k) for row k as well (since rectangle includes column sigma(k) for all rows between i and k). But row k's hole is at column sigma(k). So (k, sigma(k)) is a hole. Since rectangle is supposed to be entirely in L, it cannot contain any hole. So contradiction. So indeed any rectangle in L can contain at most one r_i.\n\nThus lower bound for L is at least number of distinct r_i, which is n-1 (since one row has sigma(i)=1, no left cell). Similarly for R.\n\nThus total at least 2n-2 rectangles.\n\nNow, is there any possibility to use fewer rectangles by using rectangles that cross both L and R? The solution's Lemma 1 says any rectangle that contains cells from at least two distinct rows must be entirely in L or entirely in R. Actually they claim that any rectangle whose interior consists only of covered squares and contains squares from at least two distinct rows must be either wholly in L or wholly in R. Let's examine that.\n\nLemma 1: Let T be an axis-parallel rectangle whose interior consists only of covered squares and which contains squares from at least two distinct rows. Then either T ⊆ L or T ⊆ R.\n\nProof: Suppose T meets rows i ≠ k. Let a = sigma(i), b = sigma(k). Without loss, a < b. If a column of T lies between a and b, then that column would contain the hole (i, a) or (k, b). Actually they argue: If a column of T lies between a and b, then that column would contain the hole (i, a) or (k, b). Let's examine: Suppose T include