es J\\) let \\(i^{\\dagger}\\) be the row in \\(I\\) with the largest hole column.  \n   The cell \\((i^{\\dagger},\\sigma(i^{\\dagger})+1)\\) lies in the rectangle, and this cell determines the rectangle uniquely.  \n   Hence each right‑type rectangle corresponds to a distinct row with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\); there are again \\(n-1\\) of them.\n\n   Since left‑type and right‑type rectangles lie in disjoint parts of the board, the total number of rectangles in any tiling is at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient, and the assignment argument shows that fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles are impossible.  \n   Therefore the minimum number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nNumber the rows and columns of the grid by \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nC=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\n\\]\n\nthe set of covered squares.  \nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle \\(I\\times J\\) with \\(I\\subseteq[n]\\), \\(J\\subseteq[n]\\) consecutive intervals, and it must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nI\\times J\\subseteq C\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\n\\forall i\\in I:\\ \\sigma(i)\\notin J .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus a tile is a *complete bipartite subgraph* \\(K_{I,J}\\) of the bipartite graph \\(G=K_{n,n}\\setminus U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound – a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) consider the two horizontal segments of covered cells:\n\n* left segment: columns \\(1,\\dots ,\\sigma(i)-1\\) (empty if \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\));\n* right segment: columns \\(\\sigma(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\) (empty if \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\)).\n\nBoth segments are contiguous, hence each can be covered by a single horizontal \\(1\\times\\)‑length tile.  \nPlace a left‑segment tile whenever \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) and a right‑segment tile whenever \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\).  \n\nExactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) (no left tile) and exactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) (no right tile).  \nConsequently the total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll tiles are disjoint because they lie in different rows, and together they cover every cell of \\(C\\).  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary\n\nA rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) satisfying (1) is of one of the two *types*:\n\n* **left‑type**: \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,\\min_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)-1\\}\\); i.e. all its columns lie strictly left of the hole of each of its rows.\n* **right‑type**: \\(J\\subseteq\\{\\max_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); i.e. all its columns lie strictly right of the hole of each of its rows.\n\nThe two types are disjoint because a rectangle cannot be simultaneously left‑ and right‑type.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1. Left‑type rectangles\n\nLet \\(R=I\\times J\\) be a left‑type rectangle and let  \n\n\\[\ni^{\\*}=\\operatorname*{arg\\,min}_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,\\sigma(i^{\\*})-1\\}\\), the cell  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(i^{\\*},\\sigma(i^{\\*})-1\\bigr)\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(R\\).  \nConversely, if a row \\(i\\) contains the cell (4) in some left‑type rectangle, then \\(\\sigma(i)\\) is the smallest hole among the rows of that rectangle; hence the rectangle is uniquely determined by the row \\(i\\).\n\nThus the map  \n\n\\[\n\\text{left‑type rectangle }R\\longmapsto i^{\\*}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nis injective, and its image consists precisely of those rows with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) (the rows whose left segment is non‑empty).  \nSince the permutation \\(\\sigma\\) has exactly one index with \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\), there are exactly \\(n-1\\) such rows.  \nHence any tiling contains at least \\(n-1\\) left‑type rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2. Right‑type rectangles\n\nLet \\(R=I\\times J\\) be a right‑type rectangle and let  \n\n\\[\ni^{\\dagger}=\\operatorname*{arg\\,max}_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(J\\subseteq\\{\\sigma(i^{\\dagger})+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the cell  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(i^{\\dagger},\\sigma(i^{\\dagger})+1\\bigr)\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nlies in \\(R\\).  \nAs before, the association  \n\n\\[\n\\text{right‑type rectangle }R\\longmapsto i^{\\dagger}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nis injective, and its image consists of all rows with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) (the rows whose right segment is non‑empty).  \nExactly one index satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); therefore there are \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\).  \nConsequently any tiling contains at least \\(n-1\\) right‑type rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3. Total number of rectangles\n\nLeft‑type and right‑type rectangles occupy disjoint parts of the board, so the total number \\(t\\) of rectangles in any tiling satisfies  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ge 2n-2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Exact value\n\nFrom (3) and (9) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
