e set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Critical cells\n\nFor every row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1) \\qquad\\text{(the rightmost covered square left of the hole)}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor every row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1) \\qquad\\text{(the leftmost covered square right of the hole)}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the columns \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) occurring in the set \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) are all distinct, and similarly the columns \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) occurring in \\(\\{R_i\\}\\) are all distinct.  \nExactly one index satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\); for that row no \\(L_i\\) exists.  \nExactly one index satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); for that row no \\(R_i\\) exists.  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n|\\{L_i\\}|=n-1,\\qquad |\\{R_i\\}|=n-1,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nso there are \\(2n-2\\) critical cells in total.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma: a tile contains at most one critical cell\n\nA **tile** is a rectangle \\(T=I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are intervals of consecutive rows and columns, and \\(T\\cap U=\\varnothing\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(T\\) contains two distinct left\u2011critical cells \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nWithout loss of generality \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(j)\\).  \nSince \\(L_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) and \\(L_j=(j,\\sigma(j)-1)\\) belong to \\(T\\), the column interval \\(J\\) contains both \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)-1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma(i)-1<\\sigma(i)\\le\\sigma(j)-1\\), the interval \\(J\\) also contains the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nThus the square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\in U\\) lies in \\(T\\), contradicting the definition of a tile.  \nHence no tile can contain two distinct left\u2011critical cells.\n\nThe same argument, with the roles of \u201cleft\u201d and \u201cright\u2019\u2019 interchanged, shows that a tile cannot contain two distinct right\u2011critical cells.\n\nFinally, suppose a tile contained a left\u2011critical cell \\(L_i\\) and a right\u2011critical cell \\(R_j\\).  \nThen \\(J\\) contains the columns \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)+1\\).  \nIf \\(\\sigma(i)\\le\\sigma(j)\\), the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) lies between them; if \\(\\sigma(j)<\\sigma(i)\\), the column \\(\\sigma(j)\\) lies between them.  \nIn either case \\(J\\) contains a column belonging to an uncovered square, again a contradiction.  \nTherefore a tile cannot contain a left\u2011critical cell together with a right\u2011critical cell.\n\nThus **every tile contains at most one of the \\(2n-2\\) critical cells**. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nAll critical cells must be covered, because they are ordinary unit squares of the board.  \nBy the Lemma, each tile can cover at most one critical cell.  \nHence any tiling of the board must use at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2n-2.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) construct the following horizontal tiles:\n\n* If \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\), place the tile  \n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text{L}}=\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\sigma(i)-1\\}.\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n* If \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\), place the tile  \n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text{R}}=\\{i\\}\\times\\{\\sigma(i)+1,\\sigma(i)+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\nThese tiles lie entirely in row \\(i\\); therefore they are pairwise disjoint.  \nEvery square of the board that is not in \\(U\\) belongs either to the left side or to the right side of the hole in its row, so it is covered by exactly one of the tiles defined above.  \n\nExactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) (no left tile) and exactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) (no right tile).  Consequently the total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{i:\\sigma(i)>1\\}\\bigr|+\\bigl|\\{i:\\sigma(i)<n\\}\\bigr|\n=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (5) we have a lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and from (8) we have a construction attaining this bound.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
