ngle cannot contain any uncovered square. So we need to partition the covered squares into rectangles that avoid the uncovered squares.\n\nThe solution's approach: define left and right critical cells adjacent to each uncovered square (except at boundaries). Show each tile can contain at most one such critical cell. There are 2n-2 critical cells, so at least that many tiles. Then they construct a tiling achieving exactly 2n-2 tiles: for each row, they place a horizontal tile covering all squares left of the hole, and a tile covering all squares right of the hole. That yields exactly 2n-2 tiles because there is one row where hole is at column 1 (no left tile) and one row where hole is at column n (no right tile). So total tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2.\n\nThus the answer 2n-2 = 4048 is correct.\n\nWe need to verify the reasoning: The solution uses valid methods: combinatorial reasoning, case analysis, construction, proof by contradiction (in lemma). All are valid. The lemma uses a contradiction argument: if a tile contains two left-critical cells, then it would contain an uncovered square, which is not allowed. That's valid.\n\nThe solution also uses the concept of \"critical cells\" and shows each tile can contain at most one. That seems correct.\n\nPotential issues: Are there any hidden assumptions? Let's examine the lemma more carefully: They define left-critical cell L_i = (i, sigma(i)-1) for rows where sigma(i) > 1. Similarly right-critical cell R_i = (i, sigma(i)+1) for rows where sigma(i) < n. They claim that columns sigma(i)-1 for left-critical cells are all distinct. Since sigma is a permutation, sigma(i) are distinct across rows. So sigma(i)-1 are distinct except possibly if sigma(i) = 1 for some i, but those rows are excluded. So yes, distinct. Similarly sigma(i)+1 are distinct for rows where sigma(i) < n.\n\nNow they claim a tile cannot contain two left-critical cells. Suppose tile T = I x J (I interval of rows, J interval of columns). If it contains L_i and