ower bound \\(2(n-1)\\) and the explicit tiling with exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(\\sigma(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A set of \\(2(n-1)\\) “critical’’ squares  \n\nFor every \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\np_i=(i,\\sigma(i+1)),\\qquad q_i=(i+1,\\sigma(i)).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq\\sigma(i+1)\\), the squares \\(p_i\\) and \\(q_i\\) are never uncovered; they belong to the set of squares that must be tiled.  \nAll the \\(2(n-1)\\) squares in the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{p_i,q_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nare distinct: if \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))=(j+1,\\sigma(j))\\) then \\(i=j+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i+1)=\\sigma(j)\\), which would give \\(\\sigma(j+1)=\\sigma(j)\\), impossible for a permutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lemma – a rectangle can contain at most one square of \\(\\mathcal S\\)\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(R\\) be an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior contains no uncovered square \\((k,\\sigma(k))\\).  \n> Then \\(R\\) contains at most one element of \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the row interval of \\(R\\) as \\([a,b]\\) and the column interval as \\([c,d]\\) (with \\(a\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le d\\)).  \nBecause \\(R\\) avoids uncovered squares, for every row \\(r\\in[a,b]\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(r)\\notin[c,d].\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence either  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(r)<c\\quad\\text{for all }r\\in[a,b] \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad \n\\sigma(r)>d\\quad\\text{for all }r\\in[a,b].\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for every column \\(s\\in[c,d]\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sigma^{-1}(s)\\notin[a,b],\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nso either  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma^{-1}(s)<a\\quad\\text{for all }s\\in[c,d] \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad \n\\sigma^{-1}(s)>b\\quad\\text{for all }s\\in[c,d].\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the rectangle \\(R\\) lies entirely in one of the four “corner’’ regions determined by the permutation points:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{NW:}&\\; \\sigma(r)<c\\ \\text{and}\\ \\sigma^{-1}(s)<a,\\\\\n\\text{NE:}&\\; \\sigma(r)<c\\ \\text{and}\\ \\sigma^{-1}(s)>b,\\\\\n\\text{SW:}&\\; \\sigma(r)>d\\ \\text{and}\\ \\sigma^{-1}(s)<a,\\\\\n\\text{SE:}&\\; \\sigma(r)>d\\ \\text{and}\\ \\sigma^{-1}(s)>b.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow take two distinct squares of \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \nIf they belong to the same index \\(i\\), they are \\(p_i=(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) and \\(q_i=(i+1,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nTheir rows are \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\), and their columns are \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) and \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nThus any rectangle containing both must have row interval \\([i,i+1]\\) and column interval \\([\\min\\{\\sigma(i),\\sigma(i+1)\\},\\max\\{\\sigma(i),\\sigma(i+1)\\}]\\).  \nBoth uncovered squares \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) and \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\) lie inside that rectangle, contradicting (3).  \nHence a rectangle cannot contain both \\(p_i\\) and \\(q_i\\).\n\nAssume now that the two squares come from different indices, say \\(p_i\\) and \\(p_j\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThe row interval of any rectangle containing them includes the row \\(i+1\\) (because \\(i\\le i+1\\le j\\)).  \nThe column interval contains the column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) (since it is the column of \\(p_i\\)).  \nConsequently the uncovered square \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\) belongs to the rectangle, again contradicting (3).  \nThe same argument works for any pair \\((p_i,q_j)\\) or \\((q_i,q_j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \n\nThus no rectangle avoiding uncovered squares can contain two distinct elements of \\(\\mathcal S\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEach tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle that contains no uncovered square.  \nBy the Lemma, each tile can cover **at most one** square of the set \\(\\mathcal S\\), which has cardinality  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S|=2(n-1).\n\\]\n\nTherefore any tiling of the board satisfying the row‑and‑column condition needs at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \nConsider the two regions:\n\n* **Above the diagonal** (\\(i<j\\)).  \n  For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal tile  \n\n  \\[\n  H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n  which is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle.\n\n* **Below the diagonal** (\\(i>j\\)).  \n  For each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a vertical tile  \n\n  \\[\n  V_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\le i\\le n\\},\n  \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n\n  which is an \\((n-j+1)\\times 1\\) rectangle.\n\nThe \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles cover all squares strictly above the diagonal, the \\(n-1\\) vertical tiles cover all squares strictly below the diagonal, and the diagonal squares remain uncovered.  \nAll tiles are pairwise disjoint, and their total number is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound (8) shows that no arrangement can be tiled with fewer than \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.  \nThe construction of §4 shows that \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
