ows where sigma(s) < c, making those squares in R. However, Lemma 1 says rectangle must be entirely in R or L. So if rectangle contains any row where sigma(r) > d, then rectangle cannot also contain any row where sigma(s) < c (since that would place rectangle in both L and R). So rectangle must be entirely in L. Then indeed rectangle cannot contain any T point because T points are to the right of sigma(i). So the argument is okay.\n\nThus no critical errors.\n\nNow check if any missing justification: The solution does not explicitly prove that the lower bound for L is analogous to R. They just claim \"The same argument as in Lemma 2 (applied to the region L) shows that a tile lying in L can contain at most one element of T'.\" That's acceptable as a brief statement; it's a justification gap but not a critical error.\n\nThus Level 3 issues: Justification gaps: some steps are brief, but logic is sound.\n\nThus final verdict PASS.\n\nNow produce JSON.\n\nWe need to include \"issues\" as array. Should we include both justification gaps? The instructions: \"List any presentation issues found (Justification Gaps or Critical Errors in logic chain)\". So we list the justification gaps.\n\nPotential issues:\n\n1. In Lemma 1, ambiguous notation \"R\" used for both region and rectangle, and the proof skips explicit justification that sigma(r) < c for all rows r in [a,b]; but it's inferred.\n\n2. In Lemma 2, the proof is somewhat terse and could be clearer about why sigma(i)+1 >= c and sigma(i) < c implies sigma(i)+1 = c, but it's okay.\n\n3. The construction description uses nonstandard notation for H_i and V_j, but conveys correct idea.\n\nThus we can list these as justification gaps.\n\nNow set confidence: 0.99.\n\nNow produce final JSON.",
