ncide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation\n\\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered by axis‑parallel rectangular tiles, no two tiles\noverlapping.  Denote the set of squares to be tiled by  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j\\neq\\sigma(i)\\}=([n]\\times[n])\\setminus U .\n\\]\n\nA tile is a set of the form  \n\n\\[\nR=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\qquad (1\\le a\\le b\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le d\\le n),\n\\]\n\nand it is admissible iff  \n\n\\[\nR\\cap U=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe must partition \\(T\\) into admissible rectangles and minimise the number of parts.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) consider the square  \n\n\\[\nL_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) such squares, because precisely one row satisfies\n\\(\\sigma(i)=1\\).\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{If a rectangle }R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\text{ contains }L_i,\n\\text{ then }d=\\sigma(i)-1.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Since \\(L_i\\in R\\), we have \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\le d\\).\nIf \\(d\\ge\\sigma(i)\\), then column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) also belongs to \\([c,d]\\); together\nwith row \\(i\\) this would give the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\in R\\), contradicting (1).\nHence \\(d\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\).  Because \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\in[c,d]\\), we must have\n\\(d=\\sigma(i)-1\\). ∎\n\nConsequently a rectangle that contains \\(L_i\\) has a **unique rightmost column**\n\\(\\sigma(i)-1\\).  If a rectangle contained two distinct squares \\(L_i\\) and\n\\(L_{i'}\\) then its rightmost column would have to equal both\n\\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')-1\\), forcing \\(\\sigma(i)=\\sigma(i')\\), impossible.\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Each admissible rectangle contains at most one square of the form }L_i .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince there are \\(n-1\\) squares \\(L_i\\), any partition of \\(T\\) needs at least\n\\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nAn entirely symmetric argument applied to the squares  \n\n\\[\nR_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\qquad(\\sigma(i)<n)\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(which also number \\(n-1\\)) shows that each rectangle contains at most one such\n\\(R_i\\).  Hence at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover the\nright‑hand part of the board.\n\nAdding the two estimates we obtain the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any tiling uses at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) we place up to two horizontal tiles:\n\n* If \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) we place the tile  \n\n  \\[\n  H_i^{\\text{left}}=[i,i]\\times[1,\\sigma(i)-1],\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n  covering the whole left interval of that row.\n\n* If \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) we place the tile  \n\n  \\[\n  H_i^{\\text{right}}=[i,i]\\times[\\sigma(i)+1,n],\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\n  covering the whole right interval of that row.\n\nThese tiles are disjoint because they occupy different rows, and by construction\nnone of them contains the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  Every square of \\(T\\) lies in\nexactly one of the tiles, so they form a valid tiling.\n\nThe number of tiles used is the number of non‑empty intervals.  Exactly one\nrow has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) (no left interval) and exactly one row has\n\\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) (no right interval).  Hence the total number of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nThe lower bound (6) and the explicit construction (9) coincide; therefore the\nminimum possible number of tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board under the given\nconditions is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the problem’s board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Hence the minimum number of tiles Matilda must place is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
