ich is a known theorem, valid. They used a simple argument about removing an edge reduces needed parts by at most one, which is a logical argument. So all methods are valid.\n\nThus Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. We need to check for justification gaps or critical errors. The solution seems fairly rigorous, but there may be some gaps:\n\n- In the explicit tiling construction, they claim \"These tiles are pairwise disjoint, none of them contains a hole, and together they cover every square of G.\" They don't provide a rigorous proof that the tiles indeed cover all squares of G (i.e., all squares not in the matching). But it's plausible: For each row i, they place left tile covering columns left of sigma(i) (if any) and right tile covering columns right of sigma(i) (if any). So each row i's uncovered square is at column sigma(i). The left tile covers all squares left of sigma(i) in that row; the right tile covers all squares right of sigma(i) in that row. So for each row i, the union of left and right tiles covers all squares in that row except the uncovered one. Since each tile is defined per row, they are disjoint across rows because they occupy distinct rows. So they cover all squares of G. This is a justification gap? The solution doesn't explicitly argue that the left and right tiles for different rows are disjoint (they are because rows are distinct). It also doesn't argue that the union of left and right tiles for each row covers all columns except sigma(i). But it's fairly straightforward. This may be considered a justification gap but not a critical error.\n\n- In the lower bound argument, they claim \"Removing an edge can reduce the required number of parts by at most one: a deleted edge can belong to at most one part of a partition, so at most one part can become unnecessary after the deletion.\" This is a bit informal, but it's a valid reasoning. However, they didn't provide a rigorous proof that removal of a matching edge reduces th