"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there is a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\n2. **Left and right regions.**  \n   For a fixed permutation \\(p\\) define  \n   \\[\n   L=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad \n   R=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n   The set of cells to be tiled is \\(L\\cup R\\); a tile cannot contain a hole, hence a tile can intersect only one of the two sets.\n\n3. **Intervals of rows for each column.**  \n   For each column \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le n-1)\\) put  \n   \\[\n   B_k(i)=\\begin{cases}\n            1,&p(i)>k,\\\\[2pt]\n            0,&p(i)\\le k,\n          \\end{cases}\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n .\n   \\]\n   The rows where column \\(k\\) belongs to \\(L\\) are exactly the indices \\(i\\) with \\(B_k(i)=1\\).  \n   The binary word \\(B_k\\) splits into a collection of maximal contiguous blocks of 1\u2019s; each block is a **row\u2011interval** that must be covered by at least one tile (a tile can cover only a contiguous set of rows).  \n   Analogously, for the right side define \\(C_k(i)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{p(i)<k\\}}\\) (\\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\)); its blocks of 1\u2019s are the row\u2011intervals for column \\(k\\) in \\(R\\).\n\n4. **Counting the row\u2011intervals.**  \n   An interval begins exactly when a binary word changes from 0 to 1, or at the first row if the word starts with 1.  \n   - For the left side, the number of intervals equals  \n     \\[\n     (p(1)-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\max\\bigl(0,\\;p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr).\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n     Indeed, columns \\(1,\\dots ,p(1)-1\\) start a block at row\u202f1, and whenever \\(p(i+1)>p(i)\\) the columns \\(p(i)+1,\\dots ,p(i+1)\\) start new blocks at row\u202f\\(i+1\\).  \n   - For the right side the analogous count is  \n     \\[\n     (n-p(1))+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\max\\bigl(0,\\;p(i)-p(i+1)\\bigr).\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n   Adding (1) and (2) we obtain the total number of row\u2011intervals (both sides):\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,\\mathcal I(p)= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\bigl|p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr|\\,}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since each interval must be covered by at least one tile, any tiling uses at least \\(\\mathcal I(p)\\) tiles.\n\n5. **A universal lower bound.**  \n   For every \\(i\\) we have \\(p(i+1)\\neq p(i)\\); consequently \\(|p(i+1)-p(i)|\\ge1\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\bigl|p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr|\\ge n-1,\n   \\]\n   and from (3)\n   \\[\n   \\text{#tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality in (4) holds precisely when \\(|p(i+1)-p(i)|=1\\) for all \\(i\\), i.e. when \\(p\\) is monotone:\n   \\[\n   p(i)=i\\quad\\text{or}\\quad p(i)=n+1-i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the monotone permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n   - For each column \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) the rows where column \\(k\\) lies left of the hole are exactly \\(i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\); they form a single interval. Cover this interval by the vertical \\(1\\times (n-k)\\) rectangle  \n     \\[\n     L_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n     \\]\n   - For each column \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) the rows where column \\(k\\) lies right of the hole are \\(i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\); cover them by the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     R_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n   The \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{L_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint and cover all cells of \\(L\\); the \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_k\\}\\) are disjoint and cover all cells of \\(R\\). Together they cover every unit square except the diagonal holes, and the total number of tiles is\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the lower bound (4) is attainable.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   For an \\(n\\times n\\) board the minimal number of tiles equals \\(2(n-1)\\). With \\(n=2025\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{2(2025-1)=4048}.\n   \\]  \n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work for a general \\(n\\times n\\) board and substitute \\(n=2025\\) at the end.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nThus the set of uncovered cells is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nand \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right regions  \n\nFor a row \\(i\\) the cells **left** of the hole are  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\},\n\\qquad\n\\ell_i:=|L_i|=p(i)-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe cells **right** of the hole are  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\},\n\\qquad\nr_i:=|R_i|=n-p(i) .\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad \nR=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\nEvery admissible tile (axis\u2011parallel rectangle) cannot contain a hole; consequently a tile intersecting \\(L\\) is completely contained in \\(L\\), and similarly for \\(R\\).  \nHence a tiling of the board consists of a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of rectangles covering \\(L\\) and a family \\(\\mathcal R\\) covering \\(R\\); the two families are disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Row\u2011intervals for a fixed column  \n\nFix a column \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nDefine the binary word  \n\n\\[\nB_k(i)=\\begin{cases}\n1,&p(i)>k,\\\\[2pt]\n0,&p(i)\\le k,\n\\end{cases}\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(B_k(i)=1\\) exactly when column \\(k\\) belongs to the left region \\(L\\) in row \\(i\\).  \nThe set \\(\\{i\\mid B_k(i)=1\\}\\) splits into a collection of maximal contiguous blocks of 1\u2019s; each block is a **row\u2011interval** of column \\(k\\) in \\(L\\).  \n\nA rectangle covering column \\(k\\) can intersect only one such block, because its rows must form a contiguous interval. Consequently **every block of 1\u2019s in \\(B_k\\) must be covered by at least one rectangle**.  \n\nAnalogously, for the right side we define for \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\)\n\n\\[\nC_k(i)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{p(i)<k\\}}=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&p(i)<k,\\\\[2pt]\n0,&p(i)\\ge k,\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nand the blocks of 1\u2019s in \\(C_k\\) are the row\u2011intervals of column \\(k\\) in \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Counting the row\u2011intervals  \n\nA block of 1\u2019s begins either at the first row (if the word starts with 1) or at a position where the word changes from 0 to 1.  \n\n*Left side.*  \nAt row\u202f1 the words \\(B_k\\) start with 1 precisely for the columns \\(k=1,\\dots ,p(1)-1\\); hence there are \\(p(1)-1\\) blocks that start at row\u202f1.  \n\nWhen we pass from row \\(i\\) to row \\(i+1\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)) the value of \\(p\\) changes from \\(p(i)\\) to \\(p(i+1)\\).  \nIf \\(p(i+1)>p(i)\\) then for each column  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{p(i)+1,\\dots ,p(i+1)\\}\n\\]\n\nthe word \\(B_k\\) changes from 0 (at row \\(i\\)) to 1 (at row \\(i+1\\)); thus exactly \\(\\max\\bigl(0,\\;p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr)\\) new blocks start at row \\(i+1\\).  \n\nSumming over all rows we obtain the total number of left\u2011side blocks:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; (p(1)-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\max\\bigl(0,\\;p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr)\\; } .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n*Right side.*  \nThe same reasoning applied to the words \\(C_k\\) yields the total number of right\u2011side blocks:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; (n-p(1))+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\max\\bigl(0,\\;p(i)-p(i+1)\\bigr)\\; } .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n*Both sides together.*  \nAdding (4.1) and (4.2) gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal I(p)\n   &=\\bigl(p(1)-1\\bigr)+\\bigl(n-p(1)\\bigr)\n     +\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\Bigl(\\max\\bigl(0,\\;p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr)\n                           +\\max\\bigl(0,\\;p(i)-p(i+1)\\bigr)\\Bigr)\\\\\n   &= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\bigl|p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nThe quantity \\(\\mathcal I(p)\\) is precisely the number of distinct row\u2011intervals that appear in the left or right region.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  From intervals to a lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEach row\u2011interval must be covered by at least one rectangle, because a rectangle can intersect only a single contiguous block of rows in a given column.  \nA rectangle may cover several columns **provided that those columns have exactly the same row\u2011interval**; nevertheless one rectangle can serve only one interval.  \nHence the total number of tiles is at least the number of distinct intervals:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{#tiles}\\;\\ge\\;\\mathcal I(p)\n      =(n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\bigl|p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr| } .\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A universal lower bound  \n\nFor a permutation we have \\(p(i+1)\\neq p(i)\\) for every \\(i\\); therefore \\(|p(i+1)-p(i)|\\ge1\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\bigl|p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr|\\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\nInserting this into (5.1) yields\n\n\\[\n\\text{#tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (6.1) occurs exactly when \\(|p(i+1)-p(i)|=1\\) for all \\(i\\), i.e. when the permutation is monotone:\n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\quad\\text{or}\\quad p(i)=n+1-i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nAssume the monotone permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n\n*Left side.*  \nFor each column \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) the rows where column \\(k\\) lies left of the hole are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,i\\mid i>k\\,\\}= \\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\na single interval.  Cover it by the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nL_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover the whole set \\(L\\).\n\n*Right side.*  \nFor each column \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) the rows where column \\(k\\) lies right of the hole are  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,i\\mid i<k\\,\\}= \\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\},\n\\]\n\nagain a single interval.  Cover it by  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint from each other and from the rectangles \\(L_k\\), and they cover the whole set \\(R\\).\n\nThus the \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{R_k\\}_{k=2}^{n}\\) tile the board, leaving exactly the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) uncovered.  Hence the lower bound (6.1) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Final answer  \n\nFor the original board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2(2025-1)=\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
