(i)>k,\\\\[2pt]\n0,&p(i)\\le k,\n\\end{cases}\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(B_k(i)=1\\) exactly when column \\(k\\) belongs to the left region \\(L\\) in row \\(i\\).  \nThe set \\(\\{i\\mid B_k(i)=1\\}\\) splits into a collection of maximal contiguous blocks of 1\u2019s; each block is a **row\u2011interval** of column \\(k\\) in \\(L\\).  \n\nA rectangle covering column \\(k\\) can intersect only one such block, because its rows must form a contiguous interval. Consequently **every block of 1\u2019s in \\(B_k\\) must be covered by at least one rectangle**.  \n\nAnalogously, for the right side we define for \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\)\n\n\\[\nC_k(i)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{p(i)<k\\}}=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&p(i)<k,\\\\[2pt]\n0,&p(i)\\ge k,\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nand the blocks of 1\u2019s in \\(C_k\\) are the row\u2011intervals of column \\(k\\) in \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Counting the row\u2011intervals  \n\nA block of 1\u2019s begins either at the first row (if the word starts with 1) or at a position where the word changes from 0 to 1.  \n\n*Left side.*  \nAt row\u202f1 the words \\(B_k\\) start with 1 precisely for the columns \\(k=1,\\dots ,p(1)-1\\); hence there are \\(p(1)-1\\) blocks that start at row\u202f1.  \n\nWhen we pass from row \\(i\\) to row \\(i+1\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)) the value of \\(p\\) changes from \\(p(i)\\) to \\(p(i+1)\\).  \nIf \\(p(i+1)>p(i)\\) then for each column  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{p(i)+1,\\dots ,p(i+1)\\}\n\\]\n\nthe word \\(B_k\\) changes from 0 (at row \\(i\\)) to 1 (at row \\(i+1\\)); thus exactly \\(\\max\\bigl(0,\\;p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr)\\) new blocks start at row \\(i+1\\).  \n\nSumming over all rows we obtain the total number of left\u2011side blocks:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; (p(1)-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\max\\bigl(0,\\;p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr)\\; } .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n*Right side.*  \nThe same reasoning applied to the words \\(C_k\\) yields the total number of right\u2011side blocks:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; (n-p(1))+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\max\\bigl(0,\\;p(i)-p(i+1)\\bigr)\\; } .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n*Both sides together.*  \nAdding (4.1) and (4.2) gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal I(p)\n   &=\\bigl(p(1)-1\\bigr)+\\bigl(n-p(1)\\bigr)\n     +\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\Bigl(\\max\\bigl(0,\\;p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr)\n                           +\\max\\bigl(0,\\;p(i)-p(i+1)\\bigr)\\Bigr)\\\\\n   &= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\bigl|p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nThe quantity \\(\\mathcal I(p)\\) is precisely the number of distinct row\u2011intervals that appear in the left or right region.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  From intervals to a lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEach row\u2011interval must be covered by at least one rectangle, because a rectangle can intersect only a single contiguous block of rows in a given column.  \nA rectangle may cover several columns **provided that those columns have exactly the same row\u2011interval**; nevertheless one rectangle can serve only one interval.  \nHence the total number of tiles is at least the number of distinct intervals:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{#tiles}\\;\\ge\\;\\mathcal I(p)\n      =(n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\bigl|p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr| } .\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A universal lower bound  \n\nFor a permutation we have \\(p(i+1)\\neq p(i)\\) for every \\(i\\); therefore \\(|p(i+1)-p(i)|\\ge1\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\bigl|p(i+1)-p(i)\\bigr|\\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\nInserting this into (5.1) yields\n\n\\[\n\\text{#tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (6.1) occurs exactly when \\(|p(i+1)-p(i)|=1\\) for all \\(i\\), i.e. when the permutation is monotone:\n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\quad\\text{or}\\quad p(i)=n+1-i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nAssume the monotone permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n\n*Left side.*  \nFor each column \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) the rows where column \\(k\\) lies left of the hole are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,i\\mid i>k\\,\\}= \\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\na single interval.  Cover it by the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nL_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover the whole set \\(L\\).\n\n*Right side.*  \nFor each column \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) the rows where column \\(k\\) lies right of the hole are  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,i\\mid i<k\\,\\}= \\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\},\n\\]\n\nagain a single interval.  Cover it by  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint from each other and from the rectangles \\(L_k\\), and they cover the whole set \\(R\\).\n\nThus the \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{R_k\\}_{k=2}^{n}\\) tile the board, leaving exactly the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) uncovered.  Hence the lower bound (6.1) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Final answer  \n\nFor the original board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2(2025-1)=\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
